window.HOLUS_DATA=window.HOLUS_DATA||{};window.HOLUS_DATA["nh"]={"id":"nh","num":1,"name":"Natural Health Fundamentals","area":"Holistic health","lessons":[{"n":1,"title":"Natural Health Fundamentals","kicker":"Define whole-person health, compare models of care and make safe, evidence-aware choices about natural health products and practitioners.","steps":[{"id":"nhf1-1","chapter":"Foundations of natural health","chapterSummary":null,"title":"What natural health means","highYield":"Natural health is a whole-person orientation that emphasizes prevention, daily habits and the body’s capacity to recover—while still recognizing when diagnosis and medical treatment are necessary.","teach":["The source describes health as more than the absence of symptoms. Physical, mental, emotional, spiritual and environmental dimensions interact, so a concern may have more than one contributor. A whole-person view asks what is happening in the person’s life as well as what is happening in one organ or laboratory value.","This orientation does not make every “natural” treatment effective. A useful distinction is between a philosophy of care—prevention, participation and context—and a specific intervention, which still needs evidence for benefit, dose, safety and fit."],"keyPoints":["Whole-person is a lens, not a guarantee that a treatment works.","Prevention includes sleep, food, movement, stress skills and safer environments.","Symptoms that are severe, persistent or unexplained still require appropriate assessment."],"visual":{"title":"The whole-person lens","items":[{"label":"Body","detail":"Function, symptoms and physiology"},{"label":"Mind","detail":"Thoughts, stress and coping"},{"label":"Context","detail":"Relationships, environment and resources"},{"label":"Meaning","detail":"Values, purpose and beliefs"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"WHOLE","explanation":"Well-being · Habits · Outlook · Life context · Evidence"},"goodToKnow":"Health goals can be meaningful and personal, but treatment claims are testable. Keep those two conversations separate.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Holistic health and personal responsibility","highYield":"Personal responsibility means having agency and support to make informed choices; it does not mean blaming a person for illness.","teach":["The lesson emphasizes that daily patterns can influence health and that people can participate in their own care. Agency includes learning, asking questions, choosing realistic habits, taking medicines correctly and communicating with practitioners.","Responsibility must be interpreted with compassion. Genetics, income, housing, food access, trauma, disability and work conditions shape what is possible. A holistic approach notices those constraints instead of turning health into a moral test."],"keyPoints":["Agency grows when choices are understandable and achievable.","Responsibility is not the same as fault.","A plan must fit the person’s actual resources and context."],"visual":{"title":"From intention to action","items":[{"label":"Value","detail":"What matters now?"},{"label":"Choice","detail":"What options are acceptable?"},{"label":"Support","detail":"What would make the choice possible?"},{"label":"Review","detail":"What happened and what changes?"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"A-S-K","explanation":"Agency · Support · Kindness"},"goodToKnow":"The strongest plan is often the smallest action a person can repeat, observe and adjust.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Look beyond symptoms—without ignoring them","highYield":"Symptoms are information. Good care relieves suffering, investigates plausible causes and avoids delaying urgent assessment.","teach":["The source contrasts suppressing symptoms with finding underlying causes. That contrast can be useful when it prompts better questions: Is sleep loss driving fatigue? Is a medicine contributing? Is there an infection, nutrient deficiency or chronic disease that needs diagnosis?","It becomes unsafe when “root cause” language implies that symptoms should never be treated or that one hidden imbalance explains everything. Symptom relief and cause-directed care can occur together; sometimes the cause is known, sometimes it is multifactorial and sometimes uncertainty remains."],"keyPoints":["Treating a symptom and investigating a cause are not opposites.","Red flags take priority over lifestyle experiments.","Avoid single-cause stories for complex chronic conditions."],"visual":{"title":"Clinical sequence","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Describe timing, severity and pattern"},{"label":"Triage","detail":"Check red flags and urgency"},{"label":"Assess","detail":"Consider medical, behavioural and contextual causes"},{"label":"Act","detail":"Relieve, treat and monitor"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"S-T-A-R","explanation":"Symptoms · Triage · Assessment · Response"},"goodToKnow":"“Root cause” is not a regulated diagnosis. Ask what evidence supports a proposed cause and how it would be confirmed.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Allopathic, complementary, alternative and integrative care","highYield":"The terms describe how an approach relates to conventional care—not whether it is effective.","teach":["The source uses allopathic medicine for conventional medical care, especially diagnosis, drugs and surgery. Complementary care is used alongside conventional treatment; alternative care is used instead; integrative care intentionally coordinates suitable conventional and complementary approaches.","The same intervention may be complementary in one situation and alternative in another. A relaxation practice used during cancer treatment is complementary; replacing effective cancer treatment with it is alternative and may be dangerous. Integrative care requires communication, evidence and coordinated responsibility."],"keyPoints":["Complementary means alongside; alternative means instead of.","Integrative care coordinates rather than merely accumulating treatments.","The label does not establish efficacy."],"visual":{"title":"Relationship to standard care","items":[{"label":"Conventional","detail":"Established medical diagnosis and treatment"},{"label":"Complementary","detail":"Added alongside standard care"},{"label":"Alternative","detail":"Used in place of standard care"},{"label":"Integrative","detail":"Coordinated, evidence-aware combination"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"C-A-I","explanation":"Complement · Alternative · Integrate"},"goodToKnow":"More modalities do not automatically create better care. Interactions, cost and treatment burden can increase as a plan grows.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Who practises natural health","highYield":"Titles, education, regulation and scope vary. Verify the practitioner’s credentials and match the problem to the appropriate professional.","teach":["The PDFs introduce naturopathic doctors, chiropractors, acupuncturists, herbalists, homeopaths and other complementary practitioners. Training and regulation differ by profession and jurisdiction, and a familiar title may not have the same legal meaning everywhere.","Scope matters more than confidence. A practitioner should explain qualifications, fees, evidence, risks, alternatives and referral limits. Ethical care does not promise cures, diagnose outside scope, discourage necessary treatment or create fear of all conventional care."],"keyPoints":["Check the regulator or professional college where one exists.","Credentials should match the service being offered.","Good practitioners refer when the concern is outside their scope."],"visual":{"title":"Credential check","items":[{"label":"Title","detail":"Is it protected or unregulated?"},{"label":"Training","detail":"What accredited education and supervision?"},{"label":"Scope","detail":"What may this person legally assess or treat?"},{"label":"Accountability","detail":"Who handles complaints and standards?"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"T-S-A","explanation":"Training · Scope · Accountability"},"goodToKnow":"A certificate of attendance and a regulated clinical licence are not equivalent.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Hippocrates and the history of whole-person care","highYield":"Historical ideas explain how care traditions developed; they do not by themselves prove a modern treatment.","teach":["The core reading traces holistic themes to Hippocratic medicine: observe the person, consider diet and environment, support recovery and avoid harm. It also describes how modern medicine increasingly focused on anatomy, pathogens, drugs and surgery—developments that produced major gains.","History is best used as context. An old practice may inspire a research question, but age is neither proof nor disproof. Modern decisions should combine cultural respect, biologic plausibility, clinical evidence, patient values and safety."],"keyPoints":["Historical longevity is not clinical evidence.","Conventional medicine and whole-person attention are not mutually exclusive.","“Traditional” and “modern” both contain useful and harmful practices."],"visual":{"title":"How to use history","items":[{"label":"Origin","detail":"Where did the idea come from?"},{"label":"Meaning","detail":"What problem was it trying to solve?"},{"label":"Evidence","detail":"What has been tested?"},{"label":"Use","detail":"What is safe and appropriate now?"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"O-M-E-U","explanation":"Origin · Meaning · Evidence · Use"},"goodToKnow":"Appeal-to-tradition and appeal-to-novelty are mirror-image errors: neither old nor new automatically means better.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Canada’s natural health product landscape","highYield":"In Canada, authorized natural health products carry a product licence number, but authorization is not a promise that every claim is clinically important for every person.","teach":["The industry reading describes widespread use of vitamins, minerals, herbal products, homeopathic products, probiotics and other natural health products. Statistics in the PDF reflect its publication period and should be learned as historical context, not current prevalence.","Health Canada product licensing provides a regulatory checkpoint for quality, labelling and permitted claims. Consumers should still read the indication, dose, warnings, medicinal ingredients and evidence category. A licence does not remove interaction risk or make a product necessary."],"keyPoints":["Use dated market statistics with their date.","Look for an NPN or DIN-HM on authorized Canadian products.","Regulation reduces uncertainty; it does not eliminate it."],"visual":{"title":"Read a Canadian NHP label","items":[{"label":"Identity","detail":"Medicinal ingredient and amount"},{"label":"Authorization","detail":"NPN or DIN-HM"},{"label":"Use","detail":"Permitted indication and dose"},{"label":"Safety","detail":"Warnings, interactions and storage"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"N-P-N","explanation":"Number · Purpose · Notices"},"goodToKnow":"Different evidence standards may support different types of authorized claims; read the exact permitted wording.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Bridge conventional and complementary care safely","highYield":"Disclosure protects the client. Every practitioner should know the full list of medicines, supplements and therapies being used.","teach":["The “Bridging the Divide” reading highlights a common safety gap: people may use complementary care without telling their physician, pharmacist or other practitioners. Reasons include fear of dismissal, assuming natural products are harmless or simply not being asked.","A neutral medication reconciliation prevents avoidable duplication and interaction. Communication should include why the product is used, who recommended it, when it started, dose, response and adverse effects. The goal is coordination, not ridicule."],"keyPoints":["Include teas, powders, drops and topical products—not only pills.","Pharmacists are valuable interaction resources.","Document starts, stops, effects and adverse events."],"visual":{"title":"One shared list","items":[{"label":"Name","detail":"Exact product or therapy"},{"label":"Dose","detail":"Amount, route and frequency"},{"label":"Purpose","detail":"Target symptom or goal"},{"label":"Monitor","detail":"Benefit, harm and review date"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"N-D-P-M","explanation":"Name · Dose · Purpose · Monitor"},"goodToKnow":"“My doctor knows I take vitamins” is not the same as sharing exact products and doses.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Natural does not mean safe","highYield":"Safety depends on the substance, dose, route, person, product quality and combination—not the source alone.","teach":["The regulation reading explores debate around natural health products. A useful modern frame is benefit–risk: Is the claimed benefit plausible and supported? Is the dose known? Could it interact with medicines, pregnancy, surgery, allergy or disease? Is the product accurately labelled?","Risk is not binary. A culinary amount may differ from a concentrated extract; topical use differs from ingestion. Contamination, adulteration and variable potency are product-level concerns. The safest response is not “all natural products are dangerous” or “all are gentle,” but specific evaluation."],"keyPoints":["Natural origin does not predict dose safety.","Concentration and route can change risk.","Pregnancy, liver/kidney disease and surgery deserve extra caution."],"visual":{"title":"Benefit–risk screen","items":[{"label":"Benefit","detail":"What outcome and evidence?"},{"label":"Dose","detail":"How much and for how long?"},{"label":"Person","detail":"Age, pregnancy, disease, allergy?"},{"label":"Combination","detail":"Medicines, products and procedures?"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"B-D-P-C","explanation":"Benefit · Dose · Person · Combination"},"goodToKnow":"An adverse reaction can be reported even when causality is uncertain; reporting helps regulators detect patterns.","act":null},{"id":"nhf1-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Make a safe care decision","highYield":"A good decision names the goal, compares options, checks evidence and safety, then sets a measurable review point.","teach":["Whole-person care becomes practical when it changes the decision process. Start with the problem and the person’s priorities. List reasonable options—including watchful waiting, lifestyle support, medical assessment and complementary care—then compare likely benefit, burden, cost and risk.","Agree on what success would look like and when to review. If no improvement occurs, do not endlessly add products. Reassess the diagnosis, adherence, dose, interactions and whether the approach should stop."],"keyPoints":["Begin with a defined outcome, not a product.","Compare alternatives, including doing less.","Every trial needs a review date and stop rule."],"visual":{"title":"Shared decision loop","items":[{"label":"Goal","detail":"Define the change that matters"},{"label":"Options","detail":"Include benefits, risks and costs"},{"label":"Trial","detail":"Choose a safe, time-limited plan"},{"label":"Review","detail":"Continue, adapt, stop or refer"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"G-O-T-R","explanation":"Goal · Options · Trial · Review"},"goodToKnow":"A product without a review date tends to become a permanent expense even when benefit is unclear.","act":null},{"id":"foundations-of-natural-health-match","title":"Match it: The whole-person lens","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["Function, symptoms and physiology","Thoughts, stress and coping","Relationships, environment and resources","Values, purpose and beliefs"],"items":[{"label":"Meaning","category":3,"feedback":"Meaning: Values, purpose and beliefs"},{"label":"Context","category":2,"feedback":"Context: Relationships, environment and resources"},{"label":"Mind","category":1,"feedback":"Mind: Thoughts, stress and coping"},{"label":"Body","category":0,"feedback":"Body: Function, symptoms and physiology"}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What natural health means","back":"Natural health is a whole-person orientation that emphasizes prevention, daily habits and the body’s capacity to recover—while still recognizing when diagnosis and medical treatment are necessary."},{"front":"Holistic health and personal responsibility","back":"Personal responsibility means having agency and support to make informed choices; it does not mean blaming a person for illness."},{"front":"Look beyond symptoms—without ignoring them","back":"Symptoms are information. Good care relieves suffering, investigates plausible causes and avoids delaying urgent assessment."},{"front":"Allopathic, complementary, alternative and integrative care","back":"The terms describe how an approach relates to conventional care—not whether it is effective."},{"front":"Who practises natural health","back":"Titles, education, regulation and scope vary. Verify the practitioner’s credentials and match the problem to the appropriate professional."},{"front":"Hippocrates and the history of whole-person care","back":"Historical ideas explain how care traditions developed; they do not by themselves prove a modern treatment."},{"front":"Canada’s natural health product landscape","back":"In Canada, authorized natural health products carry a product licence number, but authorization is not a promise that every claim is clinically important for every person."},{"front":"Bridge conventional and complementary care safely","back":"Disclosure protects the client. Every practitioner should know the full list of medicines, supplements and therapies being used."},{"front":"Natural does not mean safe","back":"Safety depends on the substance, dose, route, person, product quality and combination—not the source alone."},{"front":"Make a safe care decision","back":"A good decision names the goal, compares options, checks evidence and safety, then sets a measurable review point."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Many people strive to achieve natural health in their life. This emphasizes:","o":["the body, mind, and spirit","a patient’s responsibility for his/her own health","the symptoms of disease","A and B only","B and C only"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Tremendous advances have been made in allopathic medicine over the years. This medicine focuses on:","o":["surgery and drugs","preventive approaches to health","elimination of symptoms of disease","botanical remedies","A and C only"],"a":4,"e":"This is the course taxonomy; modern conventional care can also include prevention and chronic-disease management."},{"q":"Integrative health is a combined approach to medical care. This healing-oriented approach:","o":["integrates elements complementary care with conventional medicine","disregards the principles of allopathic medicine","is based on homeopathic medicine","does not include traditional healing methods","is the same as alternative medicine"],"a":0,"e":"The PDF appears to omit “of” in “elements of complementary care”; official wording is preserved."},{"q":"Complementary and alternative (CAM) practitioners include:","o":["acupuncturists","herbalists","chiropractors","homeopathic doctors","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"“Homeopathic doctors” is course terminology; professional titles and scopes vary by jurisdiction."},{"q":"People have many different ideas of what is meant by natural health. The premise of natural health is:","o":["symptoms are the key indicator of healing","causal effects are optimum indicators of a healthy body","the elimination of symptoms can be achieved by healing alternatives","the body has a natural ability to protect and heal itself from sickness","lifestyle imbalances aid in diagnosis of optimal health"],"a":3,"e":"This is a course philosophy, not a guarantee that the body will recover without indicated treatment."},{"q":"Many people feel that allopathic medicine has made tremendous advances in treating chronic illnesses such as type-2 diabetes.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"False is source-intended, but the acute-versus-chronic contrast is an overgeneralization."},{"q":"Integrative medicine acknowledges the importance of randomized, controlled-trial studies.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Only one-third of Americans are using some form of complementary or alternative medicine.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The source cites historical U.S. surveys. Do not treat the prevalence figure as current."},{"q":"Integrative medicine promotes an understanding of the body’s healing systems.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Allopathic medicine encompasses many holistic practices.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"False follows the source taxonomy; contemporary conventional practice can include prevention and whole-person care."}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":2,"title":"The Human Body","kicker":"Connect levels of organization, homeostasis and the major body systems so nutrition and lifestyle advice stays grounded in physiology.","steps":[{"id":"nhf2-1","chapter":"Body systems working together","chapterSummary":null,"title":"From atoms to organ systems","highYield":"The body is organized in nested levels: chemical building blocks form cells, cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs cooperate in systems.","teach":["Atoms and molecules create the chemical level. Cells are the smallest living units; groups of similar cells form epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissues. Two or more tissues form organs, and organs cooperate within systems.","The hierarchy prevents isolated thinking. A nutrient may affect a molecular pathway, but the lived outcome depends on cells, blood flow, nerves, hormones, behaviour and environment."],"keyPoints":["Cells are living units; tissues are organized cell groups.","Organs contain multiple tissue types.","Systems interact rather than operate as separate departments."],"visual":{"title":"Organization ladder","items":[{"label":"Chemical","detail":"Atoms and molecules"},{"label":"Cell","detail":"Smallest living unit"},{"label":"Tissue","detail":"Cooperating similar cells"},{"label":"Organ/system","detail":"Integrated functions"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"C-C-T-O-S","explanation":"Chemical · Cell · Tissue · Organ · System"},"goodToKnow":"A mechanism at one level does not guarantee a whole-person clinical effect.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Homeostasis and nervous-system control","highYield":"Homeostasis is dynamic regulation within workable ranges, coordinated through sensors, control centres and effectors.","teach":["Temperature, blood pressure, glucose, fluid balance and pH fluctuate. Receptors detect change; the nervous or endocrine system processes the signal; effectors respond. Negative feedback usually reduces the original disturbance.","The central nervous system includes brain and spinal cord. The peripheral system carries sensory and motor information. Somatic pathways support voluntary action; autonomic sympathetic and parasympathetic branches adjust organs without conscious control."],"keyPoints":["Homeostasis means regulated range, not a frozen value.","Sympathetic activity mobilizes; parasympathetic activity supports rest-and-digest functions.","Feedback language describes direction, not good versus bad."],"visual":{"title":"Control loop","items":[{"label":"Change","detail":"A variable moves"},{"label":"Sensor","detail":"Receptor detects it"},{"label":"Control","detail":"Brain, cord or endocrine signal"},{"label":"Effector","detail":"Tissue responds"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"R-C-E","explanation":"Receptor · Control · Effector"},"goodToKnow":"Autonomic branches work in balance; “parasympathetic good, sympathetic bad” is an oversimplification.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Brain, endocrine signals and behaviour","highYield":"Nerves communicate rapidly; hormones travel through blood and often act more slowly. Together they coordinate appetite, stress, sleep, growth and metabolism.","teach":["Neurons transmit electrical and chemical signals through networks in the brain, spinal cord and peripheral nerves. The hypothalamus links neural information with endocrine control, while the pituitary coordinates several hormonal axes.","Food influences brain function by supplying energy and substrates, but no single food controls mood or intelligence. Sleep, mental health, medicines, social context and disease also matter."],"keyPoints":["Neural and endocrine systems are distinct but connected.","The hypothalamus helps integrate appetite, temperature, water balance and hormones.","Avoid one-food explanations for complex behaviour."],"visual":{"title":"Two communication speeds","items":[{"label":"Nerves","detail":"Fast, targeted signals"},{"label":"Hormones","detail":"Blood-borne messages"},{"label":"Integration","detail":"Hypothalamus and pituitary"},{"label":"Outcome","detail":"Coordinated body response"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"N-H-I","explanation":"Nerves · Hormones · Integration"},"goodToKnow":"A biologically plausible nutrient–brain pathway is not the same as proof that a supplement treats a mental-health condition.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Circulation and respiration","highYield":"The respiratory system exchanges gases; the cardiovascular system transports oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, hormones, heat and immune cells.","teach":["Air moves through the respiratory tract to alveoli, where oxygen diffuses into blood and carbon dioxide moves out. The heart propels blood through pulmonary and systemic circuits; vessels distribute flow and support pressure.","These systems depend on each other. More oxygen in the lungs is not useful without circulation, and blood cannot maintain gas balance without ventilation. Anaemia, heart disease and lung disease can all produce breathlessness through different mechanisms."],"keyPoints":["Alveoli provide a large exchange surface.","Red blood cells carry most oxygen via haemoglobin.","Breathlessness is a symptom with multiple possible causes."],"visual":{"title":"Gas journey","items":[{"label":"Ventilate","detail":"Air reaches alveoli"},{"label":"Exchange","detail":"O₂ in, CO₂ out"},{"label":"Transport","detail":"Blood carries gases"},{"label":"Use","detail":"Cells consume O₂ in metabolism"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"V-E-T-U","explanation":"Ventilate · Exchange · Transport · Use"},"goodToKnow":"Shortness of breath at rest, chest pain or blue lips requires urgent assessment—not a supplement experiment.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Digestion from mouth to colon","highYield":"Digestion breaks food into absorbable units; absorption moves those units across the intestinal wall into blood or lymph.","teach":["Chewing and saliva begin mechanical and chemical processing. The stomach mixes food and uses acid and enzymes. In the small intestine, pancreatic enzymes and bile support digestion of carbohydrate, protein and fat; most nutrient absorption occurs there.","The large intestine absorbs water and electrolytes and houses a complex microbiota. Fibre influences stool bulk and fermentation. The liver processes absorbed nutrients, the gallbladder stores bile and the pancreas supplies enzymes and metabolic hormones."],"keyPoints":["Amylases act on carbohydrate, proteases on protein and lipases on fat.","Bile emulsifies fat but is not an enzyme.","Most nutrient absorption occurs in the small intestine."],"visual":{"title":"Digestive route","items":[{"label":"Mouth","detail":"Chew; salivary enzymes"},{"label":"Stomach","detail":"Mix; acid and protease"},{"label":"Small intestine","detail":"Enzymes, bile and absorption"},{"label":"Colon","detail":"Water, microbes and stool"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"M-S-S-C","explanation":"Mouth · Stomach · Small intestine · Colon"},"goodToKnow":"Commercial digestive enzymes may help specific diagnosed conditions, but broad “enzyme deficiency” claims need evidence.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Liver, kidneys and elimination","highYield":"The liver transforms and packages compounds; the kidneys filter blood and regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid–base balance and waste excretion.","teach":["The liver processes nutrients, produces bile, synthesizes proteins and metabolizes many drugs and other compounds. Biotransformation may make substances easier to eliminate, but it can also create active intermediates.","Kidneys continually filter plasma, then selectively reabsorb or excrete water and solutes. Urine is not simply “toxins leaving”; it reflects precise regulation. Sweating and bowel movements contribute to elimination, but they do not replace liver and kidney function."],"keyPoints":["Detoxification is a set of organ functions, not a single cleanse.","Kidneys regulate as well as excrete.","More urine does not automatically mean better detoxification."],"visual":{"title":"Process and eliminate","items":[{"label":"Liver","detail":"Transform and package"},{"label":"Blood","detail":"Transport products"},{"label":"Kidney","detail":"Filter and regulate"},{"label":"Gut/urine","detail":"Eliminate selected material"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"T-R-E","explanation":"Transform · Regulate · Eliminate"},"goodToKnow":"People with kidney or liver disease can be harmed by products marketed as “cleanses.”","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Immune and lymphatic defence","highYield":"The lymphatic system returns fluid to circulation, transports absorbed fats and supports immune surveillance.","teach":["Fluid leaves blood capillaries to bathe tissues. Lymphatic vessels collect much of the excess and return it to the bloodstream. Lacteals in the intestine transport dietary fat and fat-soluble vitamins, while lymph nodes filter lymph and bring immune cells into contact with antigens.","Lymph has no single heart-like pump; skeletal movement, breathing and vessel contractions support flow. Movement can assist circulation, but claims that one massage “flushes toxins” go beyond this physiology."],"keyPoints":["Lymph supports fluid balance, fat transport and defence.","Nodes are immune meeting points, not waste bins.","Movement supports flow but does not cure immune disease."],"visual":{"title":"Three lymph roles","items":[{"label":"Return","detail":"Tissue fluid to blood"},{"label":"Transport","detail":"Dietary fat from intestine"},{"label":"Survey","detail":"Immune cells encounter antigens"},{"label":"Respond","detail":"Coordinated defence"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"R-T-S","explanation":"Return · Transport · Survey"},"goodToKnow":"Persistent unexplained lymph-node swelling deserves assessment; do not repeatedly massage it without knowing the cause.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Skin, muscles, bones and movement","highYield":"The integumentary and musculoskeletal systems protect, support and move the body while participating in temperature and mineral regulation.","teach":["Skin forms a barrier, senses the environment and helps control temperature through blood flow and sweating. Bones protect organs, provide leverage, store minerals and house marrow. Skeletal muscle produces movement and heat; smooth muscle moves contents through organs; cardiac muscle powers the heart.","Muscle cramps can have many contributors, including fatigue, altered neuromuscular control, heat, dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, medicines or disease. A universal magnesium explanation is too simple."],"keyPoints":["Three muscle types have different locations and control.","Bone is active tissue, not an inert frame.","Cramps require context rather than one automatic remedy."],"visual":{"title":"Movement system","items":[{"label":"Bone","detail":"Structure and leverage"},{"label":"Joint","detail":"Permits controlled motion"},{"label":"Muscle","detail":"Generates force and heat"},{"label":"Nerve","detail":"Coordinates activation"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"B-J-M-N","explanation":"Bone · Joint · Muscle · Nerve"},"goodToKnow":"Recurrent cramps with weakness, swelling, dark urine or medication changes need evaluation.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Nature exposure and the brain","highYield":"Time in green space may support mood, attention and stress recovery, but it complements rather than replaces mental-health care.","teach":["The “Nature and the Brain” reading contrasts constant screen stimulation with restorative outdoor experience and discusses forest bathing. Current research supports associations and short-term effects on stress and well-being, while size and durability vary.","Nature is most useful as an accessible behavioural option: daylight, movement, social connection and sensory change may all contribute. It should not be presented as a stand-alone treatment for severe depression, anxiety, psychosis or suicidality."],"keyPoints":["Multiple pathways may explain benefit.","Dose and access can be flexible; a local park still counts.","Mental-health red flags require qualified care."],"visual":{"title":"Possible pathways","items":[{"label":"Light","detail":"Circadian and alerting cues"},{"label":"Movement","detail":"Physical activity"},{"label":"Attention","detail":"Reduced directed-attention load"},{"label":"Connection","detail":"Place, people and meaning"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"L-M-A-C","explanation":"Light · Movement · Attention · Connection"},"goodToKnow":"A benefit can be real even when no single mechanism explains all of it.","act":null},{"id":"nhf2-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Connect systems in a client case","highYield":"A symptom belongs to a person and may involve several systems. Map connections before selecting an intervention.","teach":["Consider fatigue with digestive change and muscle cramps. Possible contributors span intake, absorption, fluid balance, endocrine function, sleep, medicines, training, infection and mental health. A systems map broadens inquiry without pretending to diagnose everything.","Prioritize by urgency, probability and actionability. Check red flags, gather history, refer for diagnosis when needed, then use nutrition and lifestyle support within scope."],"keyPoints":["System maps generate questions; they do not replace diagnosis.","Prioritize red flags before low-risk experiments.","Measure one or two outcomes rather than changing everything."],"visual":{"title":"Case sequence","items":[{"label":"Describe","detail":"Pattern, onset and severity"},{"label":"Map","detail":"Systems and contributors"},{"label":"Prioritize","detail":"Urgency and likelihood"},{"label":"Coordinate","detail":"Refer, support and review"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"D-M-P-C","explanation":"Describe · Map · Prioritize · Coordinate"},"goodToKnow":"A long list of possible causes can increase anxiety; explain why each question changes the next decision.","act":null},{"id":"body-systems-working-together-match","title":"Match it: Organization ladder","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["Atoms and molecules","Smallest living unit","Cooperating similar cells","Integrated functions"],"items":[{"label":"Organ/system","category":3,"feedback":"Organ/system: Integrated functions"},{"label":"Tissue","category":2,"feedback":"Tissue: Cooperating similar cells"},{"label":"Cell","category":1,"feedback":"Cell: Smallest living unit"},{"label":"Chemical","category":0,"feedback":"Chemical: Atoms and molecules"}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"From atoms to organ systems","back":"The body is organized in nested levels: chemical building blocks form cells, cells form tissues, tissues form organs and organs cooperate in systems."},{"front":"Homeostasis and nervous-system control","back":"Homeostasis is dynamic regulation within workable ranges, coordinated through sensors, control centres and effectors."},{"front":"Brain, endocrine signals and behaviour","back":"Nerves communicate rapidly; hormones travel through blood and often act more slowly. Together they coordinate appetite, stress, sleep, growth and metabolism."},{"front":"Circulation and respiration","back":"The respiratory system exchanges gases; the cardiovascular system transports oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, hormones, heat and immune cells."},{"front":"Digestion from mouth to colon","back":"Digestion breaks food into absorbable units; absorption moves those units across the intestinal wall into blood or lymph."},{"front":"Liver, kidneys and elimination","back":"The liver transforms and packages compounds; the kidneys filter blood and regulate fluid, electrolytes, acid–base balance and waste excretion."},{"front":"Immune and lymphatic defence","back":"The lymphatic system returns fluid to circulation, transports absorbed fats and supports immune surveillance."},{"front":"Skin, muscles, bones and movement","back":"The integumentary and musculoskeletal systems protect, support and move the body while participating in temperature and mineral regulation."},{"front":"Nature exposure and the brain","back":"Time in green space may support mood, attention and stress recovery, but it complements rather than replaces mental-health care."},{"front":"Connect systems in a client case","back":"A symptom belongs to a person and may involve several systems. Map connections before selecting an intervention."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The human body is a complex system of anatomy and physiology. The most basic level of the human body is:","o":["the epithelial or tissue level","the chemical level","nervous tissue","organ system level","all of the above"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"The term “homeostasis” refers to:","o":["the brain and spinal cord","equilibrium in the body’s internal environment","the body’s five senses","serious interruptions in circulation","a state of poor digestion"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following work with the digestive organs to introduce substances vital for the proper digestion of food?","o":["liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","stomach, lungs, and kidneys","gallbladder, heart, and brain","spleen, lymph nodes, and liver","all of the above"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"One function of the liver is:","o":["breaking down remnants of hormones, alcohol and medications","releasing hydrochloric acid to digest food","absorbing vitamin K","producing the hormone insulin","ensuring acid-alkaline balance"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following factors can weaken the immune system?","o":["poor dietary habits","stress","surgery","lack of sleep","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Surgery-related immune effects depend on the procedure, person and time course."},{"q":"Which system defends the body from infectious, disease-causing organisms?","o":["urinary","digestive","nervous","immune","skeletal and muscular"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Melatonin, responsible for regulating the body’s internal clock and rhythms, is produced by the:","o":["lymphatic system","parathyroid gland","pineal gland","white blood cells","reproductive system"],"a":2,"e":""},{"q":"The cardiovascular system is responsible for:","o":["circulation","adjusting the body’s temperature","helping to remove cellular waste and toxins","all of the above","a and b only"],"a":3,"e":"Prefer named metabolic wastes and transport processes over vague “toxins” language."},{"q":"Henry is involved in an auto accident caused by road rage. Which of the following is responsible for his irrational behaviour?","o":["the amygdala","the pancreas","the pineal gland","sex glands (gonads)","the thymus"],"a":0,"e":"The source key is the amygdala, but behaviour arises from networks and context rather than one brain region alone."},{"q":"Which of the following is not one of the layers of the skin?","o":["dermis","redermis","epidermis","subcutaneous","none of the above"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following is not a function of the glands associated with the skin?","o":["they help eliminate waste and toxins","they lubricate the skin","they lubricate hair to keep it soft","they prevent foreign particles from entering the body","all of the above are functions of the glands associated with the skin"],"a":4,"e":"The source uses E; particle exclusion is most specifically associated with barrier and ceruminous functions, not every skin gland."},{"q":"The human skeletal system protects inner organs and ____________________________.","o":["assists in draining excess tissue fluid","acts as a lever and attachment point for muscles","acts as a shock absorber for the spine","converts chemical energy into heat and movement","is a storehouse of energy"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"In the human brain ______________________________ are housed in the limbic system surrounding the brainstem.","o":["exocrine glands","lymph nodes","learning, memory, and emotions","hormones that prime the body for action","hormones that promote the maturation of T-cells"],"a":2,"e":""},{"q":"All of the bacteria living in the large intestine are necessary for digestion.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"The sympathetic nervous system is responsible for digestion.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Proper elimination of waste and bacteria from the colon is largely dependent upon a high-fibre diet.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Fibre supports bowel function, but “largely dependent” is an overgeneralization."},{"q":"The limbic system controls the production of lymphocytes.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"The kidneys eliminate waste materials from the blood.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"The link between the immune system and emotions is the autonomic nervous system.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"The autonomic nervous system is one pathway, not the only connection among emotion and immunity."},{"q":"In women, the ovaries produce both estrogen and progesterone.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"The epidermis contains the hair follicles.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"The kidneys help regulate blood pressure and the acid-alkaline balance in the blood.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":3,"title":"Nutrition Building Blocks","kicker":"Connect water, macronutrients, micronutrients and food patterns—then place supplements and antioxidant claims inside an evidence-aware safety frame.","steps":[{"id":"nhf3-1","chapter":"Nutrients as a connected system","chapterSummary":null,"title":"The six essential nutrient classes","highYield":"Water, carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins and minerals have distinct roles and work as a network.","teach":["Macronutrients are needed in larger amounts: carbohydrate, protein and fat, with water considered separately despite its large requirement. Vitamins and minerals are micronutrients because required amounts are smaller, not because their roles are minor.","Food delivers nutrients in matrices that affect digestion and absorption. No class is optional; the appropriate amount depends on age, body size, activity, health, life stage and total pattern."],"keyPoints":["Macro describes amount needed, not importance.","Foods contain mixtures rather than one nutrient.","Adequacy, excess and balance all matter."],"visual":{"title":"Six classes","items":[{"label":"Water","detail":"Medium for body processes"},{"label":"Carbohydrate","detail":"Major fuel and fibre sources"},{"label":"Protein/fat","detail":"Structure, signalling and energy"},{"label":"Vitamins/minerals","detail":"Regulation and cofactors"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"W-C-P-F-V-M","explanation":"Water · Carbohydrate · Protein · Fat · Vitamins · Minerals"},"goodToKnow":"“Superfood” language often hides the fact that diet quality depends on the whole pattern.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Water and whole-body function","highYield":"Water is a transport medium, solvent, temperature regulator and participant in reactions; hydration needs vary with losses and context.","teach":["Blood volume, digestion, kidney function, sweating and cellular chemistry all depend on water. Intake comes from beverages and foods, while losses occur through urine, stool, breath and skin.","Thirst, urine pattern, climate, exercise, illness, pregnancy and some medicines change needs. More is not always better: extreme intake can dilute blood sodium, while heart or kidney disease may require individualized guidance."],"keyPoints":["Hydration includes food and all suitable beverages.","Needs vary; one fixed volume is not universal.","Both dehydration and overhydration can be harmful."],"visual":{"title":"Hydration balance","items":[{"label":"In","detail":"Drinks and water-rich foods"},{"label":"Use","detail":"Blood, cells, digestion and heat"},{"label":"Out","detail":"Urine, stool, breath and sweat"},{"label":"Adjust","detail":"Thirst, climate, activity and illness"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"I-U-O-A","explanation":"Intake · Use · Output · Adjust"},"goodToKnow":"Very pale urine all day is not automatically a health goal.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Carbohydrates, glucose and fibre","highYield":"Carbohydrates range from sugars to starches and fibres; structure, processing and meal context shape their effects.","teach":["Digestible carbohydrate becomes monosaccharides that can supply energy. Whole grains, legumes, vegetables and fruit also provide fibre, water and micronutrients. Refined products may be digested rapidly and can displace those components.","Fibre supports stool form, microbial fermentation and cardiometabolic health. Glycemic response is influenced by particle size, cooking, ripeness, fat, protein, acid and the person—not simply “simple versus complex.”"],"keyPoints":["Carbohydrate quality is more than sugar grams.","Fibre includes several types with different functions.","Glycemic index is a response measure, not a moral score."],"visual":{"title":"Carbohydrate fate","items":[{"label":"Digest","detail":"Starch/sugars broken down"},{"label":"Absorb","detail":"Glucose enters blood"},{"label":"Use/store","detail":"Cells oxidize; liver/muscle store"},{"label":"Ferment","detail":"Some fibre feeds gut microbes"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"D-A-U-F","explanation":"Digest · Absorb · Use · Ferment"},"goodToKnow":"A low-carbohydrate diet is not automatically low-sugar or high-quality; food selection still matters.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Protein and amino-acid renewal","highYield":"Protein supplies amino acids for tissue turnover, enzymes, transporters, antibodies and signalling; the body continually builds and breaks proteins down.","teach":["Dietary proteins are digested into amino acids and small peptides. Essential amino acids must come from the diet. Animal foods and varied plant foods can meet needs; plant proteins can complement across the day rather than at one exact meal.","Needs rise in some life stages and clinical situations, but more is not always better. Total energy, distribution, digestibility, kidney function and resistance exercise influence how protein is used."],"keyPoints":["Protein is functional material, not only muscle fuel.","Plant proteins can meet needs when intake is adequate and varied.","Energy adequacy prevents protein from being diverted extensively for fuel."],"visual":{"title":"Protein cycle","items":[{"label":"Eat","detail":"Food proteins"},{"label":"Digest","detail":"Amino acids and peptides"},{"label":"Build","detail":"Body proteins"},{"label":"Turn over","detail":"Breakdown and reuse"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"E-D-B-T","explanation":"Eat · Digest · Build · Turn over"},"goodToKnow":"The thermic effect of protein is higher than that of carbohydrate or fat, but this does not make unlimited protein a weight-loss strategy.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Fats, essential fatty acids and membranes","highYield":"Fat supplies concentrated energy, essential fatty acids, membrane components and a vehicle for fat-soluble vitamins.","teach":["Saturated, monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids differ in structure. Linoleic acid and alpha-linolenic acid are essential; longer-chain EPA and DHA can be obtained from fish, algae and limited conversion from ALA.","Hydrogenation historically created industrial trans fats, which raise cardiovascular risk. Current guidance focuses on replacing saturated and trans fats with unsaturated fats rather than pursuing a fat-free diet."],"keyPoints":["Fat is essential; type and pattern matter.","Omega-3 and omega-6 are families, not single compounds.","Replacement nutrient determines the effect of reducing saturated fat."],"visual":{"title":"Fat functions","items":[{"label":"Fuel","detail":"Concentrated energy"},{"label":"Structure","detail":"Cell membranes"},{"label":"Carry","detail":"Vitamins A, D, E and K"},{"label":"Signal","detail":"Precursors and signalling molecules"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"F-S-C-S","explanation":"Fuel · Structure · Carry · Signal"},"goodToKnow":"Fish-oil benefits are outcome-specific; a memory claim in an older article should not be generalized to all adults.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Vitamins: solubility, roles and risk","highYield":"Vitamins support metabolic processes; water- versus fat-solubility influences absorption, storage and toxicity risk.","teach":["B vitamins and vitamin C are water soluble, although the body still stores some. Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat soluble and depend on fat absorption. They can accumulate, increasing toxicity risk at high supplemental doses.","A vitamin’s role does not mean more creates more benefit. Deficiency correction, adequacy and pharmacologic dosing are different uses with different evidence and supervision needs."],"keyPoints":["Solubility helps predict handling, not every detail.","Food and supplements can deliver different dose ranges.","High dose is a treatment decision, not a wellness default."],"visual":{"title":"Vitamin decision","items":[{"label":"Need","detail":"Is there risk or deficiency?"},{"label":"Source","detail":"Food, fortified food or supplement"},{"label":"Dose","detail":"Adequate versus therapeutic"},{"label":"Monitor","detail":"Response and adverse effects"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"N-S-D-M","explanation":"Need · Source · Dose · Monitor"},"goodToKnow":"“Water soluble” does not mean unlimited amounts are harmless.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Minerals and nutrient interactions","highYield":"Minerals are elements with structural, fluid, electrical and enzyme roles; absorption depends on form, dose, status and food context.","teach":["Calcium and phosphorus contribute to bone; iron supports oxygen transport; iodine supports thyroid hormones; sodium and potassium help regulate fluid and electrical gradients. Trace minerals are needed in smaller amounts but remain essential.","Nutrients can compete or cooperate. High-dose single-mineral supplements may disrupt another nutrient. Vitamin C can improve non-haem iron absorption, while phytate can reduce absorption of some minerals without making plant foods “bad.”"],"keyPoints":["Trace means small required amount, not low importance.","Bioavailability is not the same as amount listed.","Supplement interactions matter at high doses."],"visual":{"title":"Mineral availability","items":[{"label":"Amount","detail":"How much is present?"},{"label":"Form","detail":"Chemical form and matrix"},{"label":"Modifiers","detail":"Enhancers and inhibitors"},{"label":"Status","detail":"The body adapts to need"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"A-F-M-S","explanation":"Amount · Form · Modifiers · Status"},"goodToKnow":"A single meal does not determine mineral status; patterns and adaptation matter.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Oxidative stress and antioxidants","highYield":"Reactive species are normal parts of metabolism and signalling; oxidative stress occurs when production and defences become imbalanced.","teach":["Free radicals can arise during normal metabolism and increase with inflammation, smoke, pollution, radiation and intense exercise. The body uses enzymes and dietary compounds in an antioxidant network to limit damage.","Antioxidants are not simple “radical erasers.” High-dose supplements have not consistently prevented disease and can interfere with beneficial signalling or treatment. Food-first variety provides many compounds at physiologic doses."],"keyPoints":["Reactive species are not purely harmful.","Oxidative stress is an imbalance, not a diagnosis from vague symptoms.","Food evidence cannot be automatically transferred to high-dose pills."],"visual":{"title":"Redox balance","items":[{"label":"Produce","detail":"Metabolism and exposures"},{"label":"Use","detail":"Cell signalling and defence"},{"label":"Buffer","detail":"Enzymes and dietary network"},{"label":"Repair","detail":"Remove or repair damage"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"P-U-B-R","explanation":"Produce · Use · Buffer · Repair"},"goodToKnow":"An ORAC score measured in a test tube does not predict a clinical outcome.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Vitamin D, EPA/DHA and brain health","highYield":"Vitamin D and long-chain omega-3 fats have important biology, but supplement benefits depend on baseline status, population and outcome.","teach":["Vitamin D supports calcium homeostasis and bone health and also influences many tissues. Sun exposure, latitude, skin pigmentation, age, clothing, diet and supplementation affect status. Testing and dosing should be individualized when deficiency risk is high.","EPA and DHA are membrane and signalling fatty acids. Fish or algae can supply them. Older memory-focused articles are useful for mechanism and research history, but evidence does not justify promising cognitive improvement to every person."],"keyPoints":["Vitamin D’s clearest established role is bone/mineral homeostasis.","EPA/DHA biology does not equal universal supplement benefit.","Baseline status changes the likely response."],"visual":{"title":"From nutrient to outcome","items":[{"label":"Exposure","detail":"Food, sun or supplement"},{"label":"Status","detail":"Absorption, conversion and stores"},{"label":"Function","detail":"Biologic role"},{"label":"Outcome","detail":"Population-specific evidence"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"E-S-F-O","explanation":"Exposure · Status · Function · Outcome"},"goodToKnow":"More vitamin D can cause toxicity; use appropriate dosing and monitoring.","act":null},{"id":"nhf3-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Supplements and orthomolecular claims","highYield":"Supplements can fill a demonstrated gap or provide a therapeutic dose, but they do not replace food patterns or evidence-based treatment.","teach":["The source introduces orthomolecular medicine, which uses nutrients—sometimes at high doses—to influence health. Some nutrient therapies are evidence based for specific indications; the broad philosophy does not validate every megadose protocol.","Evaluate the exact compound, indication, dose and evidence. Check product quality, interactions and upper limits. A high dose can behave like a drug and deserves the same seriousness about monitoring and adverse effects."],"keyPoints":["Judge specific interventions, not labels alone.","Correcting deficiency differs from megadose therapy.","Food-first does not mean supplement-never."],"visual":{"title":"Supplement screen","items":[{"label":"Indication","detail":"What problem is targeted?"},{"label":"Evidence","detail":"Which population and outcome?"},{"label":"Safety","detail":"Dose, interactions and quality"},{"label":"Review","detail":"Measure benefit and stop if not useful"}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"I-E-S-R","explanation":"Indication · Evidence · Safety · Review"},"goodToKnow":"“Therapeutic” is not a synonym for safe, and “natural” is not a dosing instruction.","act":null},{"id":"nutrients-as-a-connected-system-match","title":"Match it: Six classes","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["Medium for body processes","Major fuel and fibre sources","Structure, signalling and energy","Regulation and cofactors"],"items":[{"label":"Vitamins/minerals","category":3,"feedback":"Vitamins/minerals: Regulation and cofactors"},{"label":"Protein/fat","category":2,"feedback":"Protein/fat: Structure, signalling and energy"},{"label":"Carbohydrate","category":1,"feedback":"Carbohydrate: Major fuel and fibre sources"},{"label":"Water","category":0,"feedback":"Water: Medium for body processes"}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"The six essential nutrient classes","back":"Water, carbohydrate, protein, fat, vitamins and minerals have distinct roles and work as a network."},{"front":"Water and whole-body function","back":"Water is a transport medium, solvent, temperature regulator and participant in reactions; hydration needs vary with losses and context."},{"front":"Carbohydrates, glucose and fibre","back":"Carbohydrates range from sugars to starches and fibres; structure, processing and meal context shape their effects."},{"front":"Protein and amino-acid renewal","back":"Protein supplies amino acids for tissue turnover, enzymes, transporters, antibodies and signalling; the body continually builds and breaks proteins down."},{"front":"Fats, essential fatty acids and membranes","back":"Fat supplies concentrated energy, essential fatty acids, membrane components and a vehicle for fat-soluble vitamins."},{"front":"Vitamins: solubility, roles and risk","back":"Vitamins support metabolic processes; water- versus fat-solubility influences absorption, storage and toxicity risk."},{"front":"Minerals and nutrient interactions","back":"Minerals are elements with structural, fluid, electrical and enzyme roles; absorption depends on form, dose, status and food context."},{"front":"Oxidative stress and antioxidants","back":"Reactive species are normal parts of metabolism and signalling; oxidative stress occurs when production and defences become imbalanced."},{"front":"Vitamin D, EPA/DHA and brain health","back":"Vitamin D and long-chain omega-3 fats have important biology, but supplement benefits depend on baseline status, population and outcome."},{"front":"Supplements and orthomolecular claims","back":"Supplements can fill a demonstrated gap or provide a therapeutic dose, but they do not replace food patterns or evidence-based treatment."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Humans need a lot of nutrients daily to function properly. Which of the following are macronutrients?","o":["carbohydrates, lipids (fats), and proteins","proteins, vitamins, and minerals","water, lipids, and enzymes","all of the above","a and c only"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"The body is composed of ___________ percent water.","o":["90","60","70","80","none of the above"],"a":1,"e":"About 60 percent is a useful adult average; it varies with age, sex and body composition."},{"q":"Protein is an energy source for the body and ____________________________________.","o":["may be complete or incomplete","is made up of amino acids","is needed for cell building and regeneration","is the second most abundant substance in the body","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"“Second most abundant substance” depends on measurement basis and is imprecise."},{"q":"Trans fats have been in the public focus recently. These fats:","o":["are created through the process of hydrogenation","are found in many processed foods","are associated with increased risk for heart disease and cancer","all of the above","A and C only"],"a":3,"e":"The cardiovascular risk of industrial trans fat is established; the blanket cancer claim is not comparably established."},{"q":"Which of the following are fat-soluble vitamins?","o":["A, E, C, and all the Bs","A, D, E, and K","B12, C, and E","all of the above","a and b combined"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Henry has made a decision to not eat meat. He wants to, however, ensure that he is keeping his body healthy. Which of the following foods should he combine to get complete proteins?","o":["beans and peas","beans and lentils","beans and whole grains","beans and water","none of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Complementary pairing is a valid example, but plant proteins do not have to be combined in the same meal."},{"q":"Which of the following is not considered a valuable source of usable protein?","o":["white rice","soy foods","seafood","meat","dairy products"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Formed by green plants as a product of photosynthesis, _______________ is/are the most abundant compound on Earth.","o":["proteins","glucose","fibre","carbohydrates","essential fats"],"a":3,"e":"This is the source key; “most abundant compound” is too broad—cellulose is commonly described as the most abundant organic polymer."},{"q":"Why would someone who eats well need to take nutritional supplements?","o":["inefficient digestion due to aging","erratic dietary habits","environmental toxins","stress","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"This is the source answer. Stress or environmental exposure alone does not demonstrate a supplement need."},{"q":"Which of the following nutrients are found abundantly in green, leafy vegetables?","o":["vitamin D","vitamin E","vitamin B2","both A and B above","both B and C above"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following are considered accurate fat facts?","o":["Allergy symptoms are reduced when EFAs are supplemented (ideally in conjunction with protease digestive enzymes).","Fat-free diets lead to EFA deficiency, which in turn leads to many serious health conditions","Glandular and hormonal functions are supported by the work of essential fats","A and C","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"The source keys all of the above; the EFA-plus-protease allergy claim is scientifically weak."},{"q":"Essential fatty acids are required for all of the following Except:","o":["regulation of blood pressure","respiratory tract linings","circulation","brain development","immune function"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following is often described as “bad” cholesterol?","o":["low-density lipoproteins","high-density lipoproteins","prostaglandins","alpha-linolenic acid","partially hydrogenated cells"],"a":0,"e":"LDL is a lipoprotein particle, not literally cholesterol; “bad cholesterol” is shorthand."},{"q":"Essential fatty acids are manufactured in the body by the pancreas.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Saturated fat is found in both plant and animal sources","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Potassium and sodium regulate the water and acid-alkaline balance in the body.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Antioxidants fight against disease-causing free radicals","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Antioxidant biology does not mean high-dose antioxidant supplements prevent chronic disease."},{"q":"Processing, preserving, and cooking food do not lead to nutrient depletion.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Vitamin E protects the cells lining the lungs from oxidative damage.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"A physiologic role does not justify high-dose vitamin E supplementation."},{"q":"Next to calcium, magnesium is the second-most common mineral in the body.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Together with iron, copper helps the red blood cells transport oxygen.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":4,"title":"A Healthy Diet","kicker":"Build a varied food pattern, compare dietary philosophies and separate useful nutrition principles from label, detox and food-combining claims.","steps":[{"id":"nhf4-1","chapter":"Food patterns, labels and real-world choices","chapterSummary":null,"title":"Build the whole-food plate","highYield":"A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients.","teach":["A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients. The source foundation includes vegetables, legumes, fruits and berries, whole grains, nuts and seeds, with animal foods optional according to the chosen pattern.","Whole foods deliver fibre, micronutrients, fats, protein and phytochemicals together as a food matrix. Use adequacy, variety, balance and personal fit to evaluate the plate—not dietary purity."],"keyPoints":["The source foundation includes vegetables, legumes, fruits and berries, whole grains, nuts and seeds, with animal foods optional according to the chosen pattern.","Whole foods deliver fibre, micronutrients, fats, protein and phytochemicals together as a food matrix.","Use adequacy, variety, balance and personal fit to evaluate the plate—not dietary purity."],"visual":{"title":"Build the whole-food plate · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The source foundation includes vegetables, legumes, fruits and berries, whole grains, nuts and seeds, with animal foods optional according to the chosen pattern."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Whole foods deliver fibre, micronutrients, fats, protein and phytochemicals together as a food matrix."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Use adequacy, variety, balance and personal fit to evaluate the plate—not dietary purity."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BWP","explanation":"Use BWP to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"“Whole” and “unprocessed” are not absolutes. Frozen vegetables, canned beans, yogurt and whole-grain bread are processed yet can remain highly nutritious.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Organic, local and sustainable choices","highYield":"The lesson connects organic and local food with soil health, biodiversity, reduced synthetic-pesticide use and shorter food-system routes.","teach":["The lesson connects organic and local food with soil health, biodiversity, reduced synthetic-pesticide use and shorter food-system routes. Organic is a regulated production standard; “natural” is a much looser label and does not automatically mean nutritious or minimally processed.","Local food can support freshness and regional producers, but distance alone does not determine nutrition or environmental impact. A practical choice weighs availability, budget, values, food safety and the importance of eating enough produce."],"keyPoints":["Organic is a regulated production standard; “natural” is a much looser label and does not automatically mean nutritious or minimally processed.","Local food can support freshness and regional producers, but distance alone does not determine nutrition or environmental impact.","A practical choice weighs availability, budget, values, food safety and the importance of eating enough produce."],"visual":{"title":"Organic, local and sustainable choices · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson connects organic and local food with soil health, biodiversity, reduced synthetic-pesticide use and shorter food-system routes."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Organic is a regulated production standard; “natural” is a much looser label and does not automatically mean nutritious or minimally processed."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Local food can support freshness and regional producers, but distance alone does not determine nutrition or environmental impact."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"A practical choice weighs availability, budget, values, food safety and the importance of eating enough produce."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLSC","explanation":"Use OLSC to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The PDFs contain dated and sweeping comparisons. Do not imply conventional produce is toxic or nutritionally worthless; date-label historical policy examples.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Grains, legumes, produce, nuts and animal foods","highYield":"Cruciferous vegetables, colourful produce and berries contribute fibre and diverse phytochemicals.","teach":["Cruciferous vegetables, colourful produce and berries contribute fibre and diverse phytochemicals. Legumes provide complex carbohydrate, fibre and protein; soaking and cooking change texture and tolerance. Whole grains provide fibre, trace minerals and carbohydrate; identify products by the actual whole-grain ingredient, not colour or marketing.","Nuts and seeds provide unsaturated fats, protein and minerals; portions and allergy safety matter. If animal foods are used, distinguish food quality and preparation from unsupported claims that one certification guarantees a specific nutrient profile."],"keyPoints":["Legumes provide complex carbohydrate, fibre and protein; soaking and cooking change texture and tolerance.","Whole grains provide fibre, trace minerals and carbohydrate; identify products by the actual whole-grain ingredient, not colour or marketing.","Nuts and seeds provide unsaturated fats, protein and minerals; portions and allergy safety matter."],"visual":{"title":"Grains, legumes, produce, nuts and animal foods · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Cruciferous vegetables, colourful produce and berries contribute fibre and diverse phytochemicals."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Legumes provide complex carbohydrate, fibre and protein; soaking and cooking change texture and tolerance."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Whole grains provide fibre, trace minerals and carbohydrate; identify products by the actual whole-grain ingredient, not colour or marketing."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Nuts and seeds provide unsaturated fats, protein and minerals; portions and allergy safety matter."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"GLPNA","explanation":"Use GLPNA to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The source lists unreliable vitamin B12 routes such as spirulina and some plant foods. Reliable vegan B12 comes from labelled fortified foods or supplements.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Compare diet patterns without ideology","highYield":"Vegetarian patterns range from lacto-ovo to vegan; adequacy depends on the foods included, not the label.","teach":["Vegetarian patterns range from lacto-ovo to vegan; adequacy depends on the foods included, not the label. The Mediterranean pattern emphasizes vegetables, fruit, legumes, grains, olive oil and fish, with modest dairy and traditionally wine with meals. Macrobiotic patterns centre whole grains, vegetables, beans or soy, soups, sea vegetables, seasonal foods and a yin–yang philosophy.","Raw-food patterns emphasize uncooked foods and use blending, sprouting, fermenting and dehydrating; the source uses a 118°F ceiling. Low-carbohydrate, low-fat, acid–alkaline and Paleo patterns should be compared for nutrient routes, restrictions, sustainability and evidence—not ranked by ideology."],"keyPoints":["The Mediterranean pattern emphasizes vegetables, fruit, legumes, grains, olive oil and fish, with modest dairy and traditionally wine with meals.","Macrobiotic patterns centre whole grains, vegetables, beans or soy, soups, sea vegetables, seasonal foods and a yin–yang philosophy.","Raw-food patterns emphasize uncooked foods and use blending, sprouting, fermenting and dehydrating; the source uses a 118°F ceiling."],"visual":{"title":"Compare diet patterns without ideology · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Vegetarian patterns range from lacto-ovo to vegan; adequacy depends on the foods included, not the label."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The Mediterranean pattern emphasizes vegetables, fruit, legumes, grains, olive oil and fish, with modest dairy and traditionally wine with meals."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Macrobiotic patterns centre whole grains, vegetables, beans or soy, soups, sea vegetables, seasonal foods and a yin–yang philosophy."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Raw-food patterns emphasize uncooked foods and use blending, sprouting, fermenting and dehydrating; the source uses a 118°F ceiling."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"CDPWI","explanation":"Use CDPWI to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Macrobiotic, acid–alkaline, raw-enzyme and Paleo rationales include philosophical or outdated claims. Restrictive versions can create energy and nutrient gaps.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Nutrient synergy versus food-combining theory","highYield":"The source describes food-combining rules: fruit alone, protein separated from starch and limited mixing of fats and proteins.","teach":["The source describes food-combining rules: fruit alone, protein separated from starch and limited mixing of fats and proteins. The companion article notes the theory’s history, lack of convincing research and the fact that humans routinely digest naturally mixed foods.","Real nutrient interactions include vitamin C improving non-haem iron absorption, dietary fat supporting carotenoid absorption and vitamin D supporting calcium absorption. Separate an evidence-supported nutrient interaction from a rule that labels ordinary mixed meals incompatible."],"keyPoints":["The companion article notes the theory’s history, lack of convincing research and the fact that humans routinely digest naturally mixed foods.","Real nutrient interactions include vitamin C improving non-haem iron absorption, dietary fat supporting carotenoid absorption and vitamin D supporting calcium absorption.","Separate an evidence-supported nutrient interaction from a rule that labels ordinary mixed meals incompatible."],"visual":{"title":"Nutrient synergy versus food-combining theory · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The source describes food-combining rules: fruit alone, protein separated from starch and limited mixing of fats and proteins."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The companion article notes the theory’s history, lack of convincing research and the fact that humans routinely digest naturally mixed foods."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Real nutrient interactions include vitamin C improving non-haem iron absorption, dietary fat supporting carotenoid absorption and vitamin D supporting calcium absorption."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Separate an evidence-supported nutrient interaction from a rule that labels ordinary mixed meals incompatible."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"NSVFT","explanation":"Use NSVFT to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Claims that fruit rots in the stomach or that protein and starch cannot be digested together are not supported by normal physiology.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Stress, timing and emotional eating","highYield":"Stress and rushed eating can change appetite, gastrointestinal sensations and eating behaviour; slowing down can improve awareness and comfort.","teach":["Stress and rushed eating can change appetite, gastrointestinal sensations and eating behaviour; slowing down can improve awareness and comfort. The lesson recommends an overnight eating break and smaller, more frequent meals, but timing should be individualized. Emotional eating begins with noticing: am I physically hungry, or am I responding to stress, sadness, boredom or loneliness?","Build a wider coping menu—movement, connection, rest, a hobby or a pause—rather than treating food comfort as a moral failure. The article offers whole-food substitutions and examples such as papaya, brown rice, avocado and salmon; use them as options, not prescriptions."],"keyPoints":["The lesson recommends an overnight eating break and smaller, more frequent meals, but timing should be individualized.","Emotional eating begins with noticing: am I physically hungry, or am I responding to stress, sadness, boredom or loneliness?","Build a wider coping menu—movement, connection, rest, a hobby or a pause—rather than treating food comfort as a moral failure."],"visual":{"title":"Stress, timing and emotional eating · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Stress and rushed eating can change appetite, gastrointestinal sensations and eating behaviour; slowing down can improve awareness and comfort."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The lesson recommends an overnight eating break and smaller, more frequent meals, but timing should be individualized."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Emotional eating begins with noticing: am I physically hungry, or am I responding to stress, sadness, boredom or loneliness?"},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Build a wider coping menu—movement, connection, rest, a hobby or a pause—rather than treating food comfort as a moral failure."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"STEE","explanation":"Use STEE to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Meal frequency and strict clock cut-offs are not universally superior. Eating for comfort can be normal; focus on distress, loss of control, adequacy and goals.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Decode natural, additives and labels","highYield":"“Natural” is not equivalent to organic, minimally processed, safer or healthier.","teach":["“Natural” is not equivalent to organic, minimally processed, safer or healthier. Read the ingredient list and nutrition information to determine what a product actually contains. The source surveys preservatives, colours, sweeteners, emulsifiers and fats; each additive needs its own dose, exposure and susceptible-population assessment.","A naturally sourced additive can perform the same technological role as a synthetic one; origin alone does not determine risk. Processed-meat examples such as cultured celery extract show why claim language and actual chemistry should both be examined."],"keyPoints":["Read the ingredient list and nutrition information to determine what a product actually contains.","The source surveys preservatives, colours, sweeteners, emulsifiers and fats; each additive needs its own dose, exposure and susceptible-population assessment.","A naturally sourced additive can perform the same technological role as a synthetic one; origin alone does not determine risk."],"visual":{"title":"Decode natural, additives and labels · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"“Natural” is not equivalent to organic, minimally processed, safer or healthier."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Read the ingredient list and nutrition information to determine what a product actually contains."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source surveys preservatives, colours, sweeteners, emulsifiers and fats; each additive needs its own dose, exposure and susceptible-population assessment."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"A naturally sourced additive can perform the same technological role as a synthetic one; origin alone does not determine risk."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"DNAL","explanation":"Use DNAL to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Several additive hazard claims in the PDFs are dated or overgeneralized. Distinguish hazard from risk and use current Canadian rules.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"GM foods, pesticides, antibiotics and irradiation","highYield":"Genetic engineering and traditional cross-breeding are different methods; the lesson’s common Canadian crop examples are corn, canola, soy and sugar beet.","teach":["Genetic engineering and traditional cross-breeding are different methods; the lesson’s common Canadian crop examples are corn, canola, soy and sugar beet. Organic standards prohibit genetically engineered seed or ingredients, while contamination and coexistence raise farm-policy questions. Pesticide use affects workers, ecosystems and residues; exposure and actual risk must be assessed rather than assumed.","Agricultural antibiotic use connects to antimicrobial-resistance concerns. Food irradiation reduces selected pathogens and pests and does not make food radioactive."],"keyPoints":["Organic standards prohibit genetically engineered seed or ingredients, while contamination and coexistence raise farm-policy questions.","Pesticide use affects workers, ecosystems and residues; exposure and actual risk must be assessed rather than assumed.","Agricultural antibiotic use connects to antimicrobial-resistance concerns."],"visual":{"title":"GM foods, pesticides, antibiotics and irradiation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Genetic engineering and traditional cross-breeding are different methods; the lesson’s common Canadian crop examples are corn, canola, soy and sugar beet."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Organic standards prohibit genetically engineered seed or ingredients, while contamination and coexistence raise farm-policy questions."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Pesticide use affects workers, ecosystems and residues; exposure and actual risk must be assessed rather than assumed."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Agricultural antibiotic use connects to antimicrobial-resistance concerns."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FPAI","explanation":"Use FPAI to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Crop prevalence, labelling rules and policy examples may no longer be current. Evaluate individual traits and foods rather than treating all GM food as one hazard.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Detoxification physiology versus cleanse claims","highYield":"The body continually transforms and eliminates compounds through the liver, kidneys, gastrointestinal tract, lungs and other systems.","teach":["The body continually transforms and eliminates compounds through the liver, kidneys, gastrointestinal tract, lungs and other systems. Hydration, adequate fibre, nourishing food, sleep and ordinary movement support normal physiology. The source also recommends lemon water, sweating, dry brushing, fasting and set meal timing as cleansing practices.","Ask whether a cleanse claim names a substance, mechanism and measurable outcome—and whether it introduces risk. Red-flag prolonged fasting, laxative use, severe restriction or claims to treat disease."],"keyPoints":["Hydration, adequate fibre, nourishing food, sleep and ordinary movement support normal physiology.","The source also recommends lemon water, sweating, dry brushing, fasting and set meal timing as cleansing practices.","Ask whether a cleanse claim names a substance, mechanism and measurable outcome—and whether it introduces risk."],"visual":{"title":"Detoxification physiology versus cleanse claims · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The body continually transforms and eliminates compounds through the liver, kidneys, gastrointestinal tract, lungs and other systems."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Hydration, adequate fibre, nourishing food, sleep and ordinary movement support normal physiology."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source also recommends lemon water, sweating, dry brushing, fasting and set meal timing as cleansing practices."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Ask whether a cleanse claim names a substance, mechanism and measurable outcome—and whether it introduces risk."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"DPVCC","explanation":"Use DPVCC to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"“Toxins” is used nonspecifically. Fever is an immune response, not toxin elimination; special foods and sauna do not replace hepatic or renal function.","act":null},{"id":"nhf4-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 4 test and remediation","highYield":"Complete all 23 official questions without notes, then sort misses by concept rather than score alone.","teach":["Complete all 23 official questions without notes, then sort misses by concept rather than score alone. For each answer, state the source’s course answer before reading the rationale. Where a question reflects dated or disputed teaching, retain the exam answer and add a separate evidence note.","Revisit only the relevant moment, then answer a parallel retrieval question and explain the concept aloud. Track confidence separately from correctness to identify lucky guesses."],"keyPoints":["For each answer, state the source’s course answer before reading the rationale.","Where a question reflects dated or disputed teaching, retain the exam answer and add a separate evidence note.","Revisit only the relevant moment, then answer a parallel retrieval question and explain the concept aloud."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 4 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Complete all 23 official questions without notes, then sort misses by concept rather than score alone."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"For each answer, state the source’s course answer before reading the rationale."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Where a question reflects dated or disputed teaching, retain the exam answer and add a separate evidence note."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Revisit only the relevant moment, then answer a parallel retrieval question and explain the concept aloud."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The printer PDF has no answer key; the course keys are inferred directly from lesson text and explicitly labelled as such.","act":null},{"id":"diet-patterns-match","title":"Match it: Build the whole-food plate · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients.","The source foundation includes vegetables, legumes, fruits and berries, whole grains, nuts and seeds, with animal foods optional according to the chosen pattern.","Whole foods deliver fibre, micronutrients, fats, protein and phytochemicals together as a food matrix.","Use adequacy, variety, balance and personal fit to evaluate the plate—not dietary purity."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Use adequacy, variety, balance and personal fit to evaluate the plate—not dietary purity."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: Whole foods deliver fibre, micronutrients, fats, protein and phytochemicals together as a food matrix."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: The source foundation includes vegetables, legumes, fruits and berries, whole grains, nuts and seeds, with animal foods optional according to the chosen pattern."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Build the whole-food plate","back":"A health-supporting pattern is built mainly from varied, minimally altered foods rather than isolated nutrients."},{"front":"Organic, local and sustainable choices","back":"The lesson connects organic and local food with soil health, biodiversity, reduced synthetic-pesticide use and shorter food-system routes."},{"front":"Grains, legumes, produce, nuts and animal foods","back":"Cruciferous vegetables, colourful produce and berries contribute fibre and diverse phytochemicals."},{"front":"Compare diet patterns without ideology","back":"Vegetarian patterns range from lacto-ovo to vegan; adequacy depends on the foods included, not the label."},{"front":"Nutrient synergy versus food-combining theory","back":"The source describes food-combining rules: fruit alone, protein separated from starch and limited mixing of fats and proteins."},{"front":"Stress, timing and emotional eating","back":"Stress and rushed eating can change appetite, gastrointestinal sensations and eating behaviour; slowing down can improve awareness and comfort."},{"front":"Decode natural, additives and labels","back":"“Natural” is not equivalent to organic, minimally processed, safer or healthier."},{"front":"GM foods, pesticides, antibiotics and irradiation","back":"Genetic engineering and traditional cross-breeding are different methods; the lesson’s common Canadian crop examples are corn, canola, soy and sugar beet."},{"front":"Detoxification physiology versus cleanse claims","back":"The body continually transforms and eliminates compounds through the liver, kidneys, gastrointestinal tract, lungs and other systems."},{"front":"Official Lesson 4 test and remediation","back":"Complete all 23 official questions without notes, then sort misses by concept rather than score alone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following vegetables are known to be effective in the prevention of cancer and heart disease?","o":["broccoli","cabbage","cauliflower","all of the above","both A and C"],"a":3,"e":"Course answer. Cruciferous vegetables can support a healthful pattern, but “effective in prevention” is stronger than current evidence for one food."},{"q":"Beans are used for many purposes and are a very versatile food. They:","o":["are known to significantly lower cholesterol levels","are approximately 70 percent complex carbohydrates","are helpful in the prevention of colon disorders","help control blood sugar and insulin levels","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Benefits depend on amount, preparation and the overall diet; the 70 percent figure and prevention wording are not universal."},{"q":"Grains have been the basis of the human diet for many years. They:","o":["are an excellent source of fibre","cannot be eaten without preparation","contain trace minerals","all of the above","Both A and C"],"a":3,"e":"The lesson states all three. “Cannot be eaten without preparation” is an overbroad generalization."},{"q":"Which of the following are considered legumes?","o":["rutabaga","brussels sprouts","soybeans","sunflower seeds","none of the above"],"a":2,"e":""},{"q":"Organically raised meat is a good source of:","o":["calcium","growth hormones","essential fatty acids","antibiotics","vitamin E"],"a":2,"e":"Inferred course answer. Organic certification alone does not guarantee a clinically meaningful essential-fatty-acid profile."},{"q":"The standard North American diet:","o":["emphasizes whole foods","contributes to chronic diseases","lacks hydrogenated fats","contains processed foods with artificial ingredients","Both B and D"],"a":4,"e":"“Standard North American diet” is a broad pattern label rather than a precisely defined exposure."},{"q":"Research shows that a vegetarian diet can lead to:","o":["calcium deficiencies","lower cholesterol","high blood pressure","obesity","diabetes"],"a":1,"e":"Often supported for well-planned plant-forward patterns, but outcome depends on actual food choices."},{"q":"Mary wants to avoid unhealthy foods. Which of the following might she start eliminating from her diet?","o":["margarine","butter","processed cheese","Both B and C","Both A and C"],"a":4,"e":"Course answer. Modern guidance should distinguish older trans-fat margarines from current formulations."},{"q":"Which of the following are the most common genetically engineered ingredients in food production?","o":["soy, lettuce, corn","corn, potatoes, soy","fish, potatoes, soy","canola, soy, tomatoes","corn, canola, soy"],"a":4,"e":"Historical source answer. Crop prevalence and regulation change by date and country."},{"q":"Fruits, vegetables, beans, and whole grains are a good source of:","o":["Isoflavones","Catechins","Limonene","Lignans","Fibre"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Manpreet wants to start a daily cleaning effort to improve her health. She should:","o":["not eat past 10 pm","exercise at least 5 minutes a day","eat small, frequent meals throughout the day","eat only fruit in the afternoon","Both A and B"],"a":2,"e":"Course answer. Meal frequency should be individualized; “daily cleaning” is not a standard clinical nutrition concept."},{"q":"Jan is considering adding foods to her diet that would help her body detoxify itself. All of the following foods will help with this, EXCEPT __________________________?","o":["Yogourt","Beets","Smoked Salmon","Dandelion Greens","Grapes"],"a":2,"e":"Course answer. Liver, kidneys, lungs and the GI tract perform biotransformation and elimination; no single food detoxifies the body."},{"q":"According to the theory of food combining, consuming fruit with other foods in one meal will result in:","o":["increased absorption of iron","improved flow of gastric juice into the stomach","improved bioavailability of lycopene","digestive disturbances","reduction of sugar-feeding bacteria in the gut"],"a":3,"e":"Course-theory answer only. Normal human digestion is designed for mixed meals."},{"q":"Generally vegetarians consume a diet that is:","o":["higher in saturated fats","lower in complex carbohydrates","higher in fibre","lower in phytochemicals","higher in calories"],"a":2,"e":"Often true at population level, but not guaranteed by the label."},{"q":"Healthy cereals are generally those containing whole grains that are:","o":["rolled","milled","cracked","all of the above","Both A and C"],"a":3,"e":"Milling may still yield whole-grain flour if bran, germ and endosperm remain in original proportions."},{"q":"Complex carbohydrates raise blood sugar levels just as fast as refined carbohydrates do.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Directionally sound, but glycemic response varies with structure, processing, fibre, preparation and meal context."},{"q":"Soy has conclusively been shown to improve health and prevent many diseases.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Fortified white bread is just as nutritious as whole grain bread.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Fortification replaces selected nutrients, not the full fibre and food matrix."},{"q":"Long-term consumption of meat may increase risk for colon cancer.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"The clearest evidence concerns processed meat and higher red-meat intake, not all meat equally."},{"q":"When following a raw food diet, some foods may be heated to a temperature not exceeding 118 F as this is the highest heat that enzymes are known to tolerate.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course doctrine. Enzymes differ in heat stability, and 118°F is not a universal scientific threshold."},{"q":"According to the theory of food combining, proteins should be combined with grains to ease digestion.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"False within the source theory; the food-combining theory itself is not supported by normal digestive physiology."},{"q":"Calcium is abundant in many nondairy foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True with attention to serving size, fortification and bioavailability."},{"q":"Fever during an illness is a natural way for the body to eliminate toxins.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer, but scientifically misleading. Fever is a regulated immune response, not accurately described as toxin elimination."}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":5,"title":"Supplementation and Functional Foods","kicker":"Understand food matrices, phytochemicals, fermentation and supplement evidence without turning mechanisms into cure claims.","steps":[{"id":"nhf5-1","chapter":"Food function, compounds and consumer evidence","chapterSummary":null,"title":"Functional foods and free radicals","highYield":"The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy.","teach":["The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy. Reactive species and free radicals arise during normal metabolism and can increase with exposures, injury, stress and illness. An antioxidant can donate an electron and help interrupt a damaging chain reaction without becoming dangerously unstable itself.","Oxidative stress describes an imbalance between reactive-species production and protective systems. Antioxidant activity in a test tube is not the same as a proven disease outcome in a person."],"keyPoints":["Reactive species and free radicals arise during normal metabolism and can increase with exposures, injury, stress and illness.","An antioxidant can donate an electron and help interrupt a damaging chain reaction without becoming dangerously unstable itself.","Oxidative stress describes an imbalance between reactive-species production and protective systems."],"visual":{"title":"Functional foods and free radicals · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Reactive species and free radicals arise during normal metabolism and can increase with exposures, injury, stress and illness."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"An antioxidant can donate an electron and help interrupt a damaging chain reaction without becoming dangerously unstable itself."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Oxidative stress describes an imbalance between reactive-species production and protective systems."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FFFR","explanation":"Use FFFR to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Reactive species also have normal signalling roles. Avoid the simple chain “oxidation causes disease, therefore more antioxidant supplement is always better.”","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Colour chemistry and phytochemical families","highYield":"Phytochemicals are plant compounds that contribute colour, flavour, aroma and biological activity; they are not vitamins or minerals.","teach":["Phytochemicals are plant compounds that contribute colour, flavour, aroma and biological activity; they are not vitamins or minerals. Examples include allicin in alliums, carotenoids in orange, red and dark-green produce, flavonoids in berries, tea, apples and onions, and indoles or isothiocyanates in crucifers. Plant sterols and stanols, lignans, isoflavones, saponins and terpenes are additional source categories.","Colour variety is a practical cue for dietary diversity, not a guarantee that a food prevents disease. Build meals with multiple plant colours across the week rather than chasing a single superfood."],"keyPoints":["Examples include allicin in alliums, carotenoids in orange, red and dark-green produce, flavonoids in berries, tea, apples and onions, and indoles or isothiocyanates in crucifers.","Plant sterols and stanols, lignans, isoflavones, saponins and terpenes are additional source categories.","Colour variety is a practical cue for dietary diversity, not a guarantee that a food prevents disease."],"visual":{"title":"Colour chemistry and phytochemical families · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Phytochemicals are plant compounds that contribute colour, flavour, aroma and biological activity; they are not vitamins or minerals."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Examples include allicin in alliums, carotenoids in orange, red and dark-green produce, flavonoids in berries, tea, apples and onions, and indoles or isothiocyanates in crucifers."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Plant sterols and stanols, lignans, isoflavones, saponins and terpenes are additional source categories."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Colour variety is a practical cue for dietary diversity, not a guarantee that a food prevents disease."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"CCPF","explanation":"Use CCPF to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Several cancer-prevention statements rely on mechanistic or early research. Describe potential roles without claiming isolated phytochemicals prevent or treat cancer.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Whole foods, fibre and the food matrix","highYield":"The source contrasts whole foods with foods stripped by refining, bleaching or peeling; modern practice treats processing as a continuum.","teach":["The source contrasts whole foods with foods stripped by refining, bleaching or peeling; modern practice treats processing as a continuum. Insoluble fibre supports stool bulk and regularity; soluble and viscous fibres can slow absorption and help lower LDL cholesterol. Fibre-rich foods include vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains, nuts and seeds.","Edible peels often add fibre and phytochemicals; wash produce and adapt to tolerance and safety needs. A whole-food matrix combines nutrients and compounds in proportions an isolated supplement does not reproduce."],"keyPoints":["Insoluble fibre supports stool bulk and regularity; soluble and viscous fibres can slow absorption and help lower LDL cholesterol.","Fibre-rich foods include vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains, nuts and seeds.","Edible peels often add fibre and phytochemicals; wash produce and adapt to tolerance and safety needs."],"visual":{"title":"Whole foods, fibre and the food matrix · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The source contrasts whole foods with foods stripped by refining, bleaching or peeling; modern practice treats processing as a continuum."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Insoluble fibre supports stool bulk and regularity; soluble and viscous fibres can slow absorption and help lower LDL cholesterol."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Fibre-rich foods include vegetables, fruit, legumes, whole grains, nuts and seeds."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Edible peels often add fibre and phytochemicals; wash produce and adapt to tolerance and safety needs."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"WFFFM","explanation":"Use WFFFM to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The source phrase “fibre absorbs fats and sugars” is too broad. Fibre types differ in viscosity, fermentability and effects.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Fermentation: live cultures versus vinegar pickling","highYield":"Lactic-acid fermentation uses microorganisms to transform food; examples include selected yogurt, kefir, sauerkraut and miso products.","teach":["Lactic-acid fermentation uses microorganisms to transform food; examples include selected yogurt, kefir, sauerkraut and miso products. Brine fermentation produces acids through microbial activity, while vinegar pickling adds acetic acid directly. A fermented food is not automatically probiotic: the organism must remain alive in an adequate amount and have evidence for a benefit.","Heat or pasteurization can remove live cultures even though the food remains fermented. Read the label and storage instructions; identify whether the goal is flavour, preservation, live culture or a studied probiotic."],"keyPoints":["Brine fermentation produces acids through microbial activity, while vinegar pickling adds acetic acid directly.","A fermented food is not automatically probiotic: the organism must remain alive in an adequate amount and have evidence for a benefit.","Heat or pasteurization can remove live cultures even though the food remains fermented."],"visual":{"title":"Fermentation: live cultures versus vinegar pickling · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Lactic-acid fermentation uses microorganisms to transform food; examples include selected yogurt, kefir, sauerkraut and miso products."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Brine fermentation produces acids through microbial activity, while vinegar pickling adds acetic acid directly."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"A fermented food is not automatically probiotic: the organism must remain alive in an adequate amount and have evidence for a benefit."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Heat or pasteurization can remove live cultures even though the food remains fermented."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FLCVV","explanation":"Use FLCVV to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not promise that fermented foods prevent infection or cancer. Home fermentation requires validated food-safety methods.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Functional-food evidence map","highYield":"Map food to active component to proposed outcome, then rate the strength of the human evidence.","teach":["Map food to active component to proposed outcome, then rate the strength of the human evidence. Examples with stronger specific claims include oat or barley beta-glucan and psyllium for lowering LDL cholesterol. Tomato lycopene, tea catechins, soy isoflavones and cranberry proanthocyanidins illustrate promising but outcome-specific research.","ORAC measures antioxidant activity in a laboratory system; it does not rank total nutritional value or predict a clinical result. Prefer a varied food pattern before isolated antioxidant supplementation."],"keyPoints":["Examples with stronger specific claims include oat or barley beta-glucan and psyllium for lowering LDL cholesterol.","Tomato lycopene, tea catechins, soy isoflavones and cranberry proanthocyanidins illustrate promising but outcome-specific research.","ORAC measures antioxidant activity in a laboratory system; it does not rank total nutritional value or predict a clinical result."],"visual":{"title":"Functional-food evidence map · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Map food to active component to proposed outcome, then rate the strength of the human evidence."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Examples with stronger specific claims include oat or barley beta-glucan and psyllium for lowering LDL cholesterol."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Tomato lycopene, tea catechins, soy isoflavones and cranberry proanthocyanidins illustrate promising but outcome-specific research."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"ORAC measures antioxidant activity in a laboratory system; it does not rank total nutritional value or predict a clinical result."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FE","explanation":"Use FE to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The source table sometimes groups mechanisms, observational findings and clinical outcomes together. Keep association, biomarker change and disease prevention separate.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Supplements, botanicals and nutraceuticals","highYield":"Dietary supplements include vitamins, minerals, herbs or extracts, proteins and fatty acids intended to supplement the diet.","teach":["Dietary supplements include vitamins, minerals, herbs or extracts, proteins and fatty acids intended to supplement the diet. The lesson profiles ashwagandha, CoQ10, EFAs, ginkgo, ginseng, glutathione, grapeseed, green foods, melatonin and pine-bark extract. A nutraceutical is described in the source as a food modified to add a nutrient or remove a component, such as calcium-fortified juice.","Classify each product by what it is: nutrient, hormone, herb, microorganism, fatty acid or modified food. For every product ask purpose, dose, formulation, duration, interactions, contraindications and monitoring."],"keyPoints":["The lesson profiles ashwagandha, CoQ10, EFAs, ginkgo, ginseng, glutathione, grapeseed, green foods, melatonin and pine-bark extract.","A nutraceutical is described in the source as a food modified to add a nutrient or remove a component, such as calcium-fortified juice.","Classify each product by what it is: nutrient, hormone, herb, microorganism, fatty acid or modified food."],"visual":{"title":"Supplements, botanicals and nutraceuticals · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Dietary supplements include vitamins, minerals, herbs or extracts, proteins and fatty acids intended to supplement the diet."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The lesson profiles ashwagandha, CoQ10, EFAs, ginkgo, ginseng, glutathione, grapeseed, green foods, melatonin and pine-bark extract."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"A nutraceutical is described in the source as a food modified to add a nutrient or remove a component, such as calcium-fortified juice."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Classify each product by what it is: nutrient, hormone, herb, microorganism, fatty acid or modified food."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"SBN","explanation":"Use SBN to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Many disease-treatment indications are historical or weakly supported. “Used for” does not mean proven effective.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Consumer detective: evidence, labels and fairy dust","highYield":"Ask whether a claim comes from human trials, animal work or test-tube research and whether findings were replicated and peer reviewed.","teach":["Ask whether a claim comes from human trials, animal work or test-tube research and whether findings were replicated and peer reviewed. Check the exact dose and formulation studied instead of relying on an ingredient name on the front label. Reject cure-all claims and products promising major weight loss without broader behaviour change.","A kitchen-sink formula may list many ingredients while providing subtherapeutic fairy-dusted amounts. Supplements complement—not replace—adequate food, diagnosis or necessary treatment."],"keyPoints":["Check the exact dose and formulation studied instead of relying on an ingredient name on the front label.","Reject cure-all claims and products promising major weight loss without broader behaviour change.","A kitchen-sink formula may list many ingredients while providing subtherapeutic fairy-dusted amounts."],"visual":{"title":"Consumer detective: evidence, labels and fairy dust · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Ask whether a claim comes from human trials, animal work or test-tube research and whether findings were replicated and peer reviewed."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Check the exact dose and formulation studied instead of relying on an ingredient name on the front label."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Reject cure-all claims and products promising major weight loss without broader behaviour change."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"A kitchen-sink formula may list many ingredients while providing subtherapeutic fairy-dusted amounts."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"CDELF","explanation":"Use CDELF to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The PDF names an older Health Canada branch. Use current Canadian natural-product authorization and label information.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Needs across age, medicines and diet patterns","highYield":"Older adults may need attention to calcium, vitamin D, B12 and total intake, but supplementation follows assessment.","teach":["Older adults may need attention to calcium, vitamin D, B12 and total intake, but supplementation follows assessment. Pregnancy planning requires reliable folic acid; iron and other nutrients depend on life stage, intake and laboratory context. Long-term medicines can affect nutrient status—for example metformin and B12 or acid suppression and selected nutrients.","Vegetarian and vegan patterns need dependable B12 and deliberate routes for iron, zinc, calcium, vitamin D, iodine and omega-3 fats. Medication use, pregnancy, chronic disease and high-dose products are referral or coordination triggers."],"keyPoints":["Pregnancy planning requires reliable folic acid; iron and other nutrients depend on life stage, intake and laboratory context.","Long-term medicines can affect nutrient status—for example metformin and B12 or acid suppression and selected nutrients.","Vegetarian and vegan patterns need dependable B12 and deliberate routes for iron, zinc, calcium, vitamin D, iodine and omega-3 fats."],"visual":{"title":"Needs across age, medicines and diet patterns · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Older adults may need attention to calcium, vitamin D, B12 and total intake, but supplementation follows assessment."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Pregnancy planning requires reliable folic acid; iron and other nutrients depend on life stage, intake and laboratory context."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Long-term medicines can affect nutrient status—for example metformin and B12 or acid suppression and selected nutrients."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Vegetarian and vegan patterns need dependable B12 and deliberate routes for iron, zinc, calcium, vitamin D, iodine and omega-3 fats."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"NAAMD","explanation":"Use NAAMD to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not prescribe automatically because someone belongs to a group or uses a medicine; several blanket recommendations require individualized review.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Practice cases and teach-back","highYield":"Classify an item as whole food, fermented food, probiotic, supplement or nutraceutical—and explain why.","teach":["Classify an item as whole food, fermented food, probiotic, supplement or nutraceutical—and explain why. Build a one-day colour-diversity plan without using detox or superfood language. Compare a brine-fermented pickle with a vinegar pickle and identify whether live cultures are expected.","Audit a supplement label for claim strength, dose, ingredients, interactions and referral needs. Teach the distinction among antioxidant mechanism, biomarker effect and demonstrated clinical outcome."],"keyPoints":["Build a one-day colour-diversity plan without using detox or superfood language.","Compare a brine-fermented pickle with a vinegar pickle and identify whether live cultures are expected.","Audit a supplement label for claim strength, dose, ingredients, interactions and referral needs."],"visual":{"title":"Practice cases and teach-back · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Classify an item as whole food, fermented food, probiotic, supplement or nutraceutical—and explain why."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Build a one-day colour-diversity plan without using detox or superfood language."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Compare a brine-fermented pickle with a vinegar pickle and identify whether live cultures are expected."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Audit a supplement label for claim strength, dose, ingredients, interactions and referral needs."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"PCT","explanation":"Use PCT to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Practice should reward calibrated language such as “may,” “associated with” and “evidence is limited,” not unsupported certainty.","act":null},{"id":"nhf5-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 5 test and remediation","highYield":"Complete all 25 official items without notes and tag each response high, medium or low confidence.","teach":["Complete all 25 official items without notes and tag each response high, medium or low confidence. Use the source answer for exam preparation, then open the evidence note for disputed claims. Group errors into free radicals, phytochemicals, fermentation, fibre, supplements and named compounds or herbs.","After review, answer a parallel question and explain the correction in one sentence. Retest missed and low-confidence items after a delay."],"keyPoints":["Use the source answer for exam preparation, then open the evidence note for disputed claims.","Group errors into free radicals, phytochemicals, fermentation, fibre, supplements and named compounds or herbs.","After review, answer a parallel question and explain the correction in one sentence."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 5 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Complete all 25 official items without notes and tag each response high, medium or low confidence."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Use the source answer for exam preparation, then open the evidence note for disputed claims."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Group errors into free radicals, phytochemicals, fermentation, fibre, supplements and named compounds or herbs."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"After review, answer a parallel question and explain the correction in one sentence."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"No official key was supplied; keys are inferred from lesson text. Several supplement and disease claims remain only as course answers.","act":null},{"id":"functional-foods-match","title":"Match it: Functional foods and free radicals · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy.","Reactive species and free radicals arise during normal metabolism and can increase with exposures, injury, stress and illness.","An antioxidant can donate an electron and help interrupt a damaging chain reaction without becoming dangerously unstable itself.","Oxidative stress describes an imbalance between reactive-species production and protective systems."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Oxidative stress describes an imbalance between reactive-species production and protective systems."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: An antioxidant can donate an electron and help interrupt a damaging chain reaction without becoming dangerously unstable itself."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: Reactive species and free radicals arise during normal metabolism and can increase with exposures, injury, stress and illness."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Functional foods and free radicals","back":"The lesson defines a functional food as a food offering potential benefits beyond essential nutrients and energy."},{"front":"Colour chemistry and phytochemical families","back":"Phytochemicals are plant compounds that contribute colour, flavour, aroma and biological activity; they are not vitamins or minerals."},{"front":"Whole foods, fibre and the food matrix","back":"The source contrasts whole foods with foods stripped by refining, bleaching or peeling; modern practice treats processing as a continuum."},{"front":"Fermentation: live cultures versus vinegar pickling","back":"Lactic-acid fermentation uses microorganisms to transform food; examples include selected yogurt, kefir, sauerkraut and miso products."},{"front":"Functional-food evidence map","back":"Map food to active component to proposed outcome, then rate the strength of the human evidence."},{"front":"Supplements, botanicals and nutraceuticals","back":"Dietary supplements include vitamins, minerals, herbs or extracts, proteins and fatty acids intended to supplement the diet."},{"front":"Consumer detective: evidence, labels and fairy dust","back":"Ask whether a claim comes from human trials, animal work or test-tube research and whether findings were replicated and peer reviewed."},{"front":"Needs across age, medicines and diet patterns","back":"Older adults may need attention to calcium, vitamin D, B12 and total intake, but supplementation follows assessment."},{"front":"Practice cases and teach-back","back":"Classify an item as whole food, fermented food, probiotic, supplement or nutraceutical—and explain why."},{"front":"Official Lesson 5 test and remediation","back":"Complete all 25 official items without notes and tag each response high, medium or low confidence."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Free radicals:","o":["are continually formed in the body","can damage cells and tissues","may cause cancer and heart disease","are increased by stress and illness","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Reactive species are continually produced and can contribute to oxidative damage, but “cause” is too direct for multifactorial disease."},{"q":"Which of the following are examples of phytochemicals?","o":["leptin","lycopene","beta-carotene","Both B and C","Both A and B"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Indole-3-carbinol is found in:","o":["soybeans","green tea","cruciferous vegetables","dark chocolate and red wine","tomato sauces"],"a":2,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following would be a good reason to incorporate garlic into the diet?","o":["It’s a potent antioxidant","It may reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease","It may reduce the risk of Alzheimer’s disease","Both A and B","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Course answer. Garlic may modestly affect selected cardiovascular markers, but Alzheimer-risk reduction is not established."},{"q":"Which of the following are found in fermented foods?","o":["lactic acid","candida yeast","miso","saponins","lignans"],"a":0,"e":"Miso is itself a fermented food; the source specifically identifies lactic acid as a fermentation product."},{"q":"Essential fatty acid supplements have been used to prevent and treat ___________________.","o":["diabetes","multiple sclerosis","inflammatory disorders","PMS","All of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Historical-use course answer, not proof of prevention or treatment for all four conditions."},{"q":"Ginseng is known as an adaptogen because:","o":["it may reduce risk for heart disease","it is recommended for arthritis and bursitis","it is used to make traditional Asian fermented foods","it is high in essential fatty acids","it can normalize and balance body functions under a variety of stressful conditions"],"a":4,"e":"This is the source’s traditional adaptogen definition, not a precise validated mechanism."},{"q":"Made from fermented soybeans, _________ should not be heated to boiling point, as its live culture will be destroyed.","o":["sourdough bread","miso","kefir","chlorella","all of the above"],"a":1,"e":"This refers to unpasteurized live-culture miso; not every commercial product contains live organisms when eaten."},{"q":"Ashwagandha:","o":["has been used to treat fatigue and chronic diseases","is derived from wild blue-green algae","is a traditional Ayurvedic medicine","Both A and C","Both A and B"],"a":3,"e":"Course answer describes traditional use. It does not prove treatment of chronic disease."},{"q":"Which of the following functional foods may reduce the risk of cancer and cardiovascular disease and may lower LDL and total cholesterol?","o":["Oats","Psyllium","Cranberries","Tomatoes","Both A and B"],"a":4,"e":"Oats and psyllium can lower LDL through soluble fibre. Direct cancer-risk reduction is less certain."},{"q":"Lisa has been told to look for whole foods when she shops. What makes these food items different is that they are:","o":["flash frozen","polished","bleached","non-processed","refined"],"a":3,"e":"Source definition. Processing is a continuum; frozen vegetables and canned beans can remain nutritious."},{"q":"Which of the following give many fruits and vegetables their colour?","o":["Phytoestrogens","Flavonoids","Saponins","Tannins","Stanols"],"a":1,"e":"Flavonoids contribute colour alongside carotenoids, chlorophylls, betalains and other pigments."},{"q":"All of the following are examples of dietary supplements except:","o":["vitamins","minerals","herbs","proteins","free radicals"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Free radicals are highly reactive molecules that can be increased by:","o":["too much sleep","illness","antioxidants","refined nutrients","All of the above"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Fibre is necessary for a healthy gastrointestinal tract. It helps to:","o":["reduce moisture in the colon","absorb fats and sugars","raise blood pressure","slow down peristalsis","all of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Course wording is oversimplified. Fibre types differ; some bind bile acids and slow nutrient absorption."},{"q":"Lactobacillus bulgaricus is found in some fermented foods.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Grapeseed extract may be used to treat arthritis.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer. Clinical evidence for grapeseed extract as arthritis treatment is insufficient."},{"q":"Melatonin is a hormone that may help the immune system.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Melatonin participates in immune signalling but is not a general immune-boosting treatment."},{"q":"Ginseng is considered an adaptogen.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True in the traditional/source classification; adaptogen is not a specific validated mechanism."},{"q":"Good sources of carotenoids include cauliflower and curcumin.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Curcumin is a polyphenol from turmeric, not a carotenoid."},{"q":"It’s best to peel fruits and vegetables before eating them","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Many edible peels contain fibre and phytochemicals; peel when required for safety, tolerance or culinary use."},{"q":"Gingko is commonly recommended for its role in preventing memory loss and is also used to increase circulation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer preserves the source spelling. Ginkgo has not been established to prevent dementia and can increase bleeding risk."},{"q":"Celery, berries, parsley and onions are good sources of flavonoids.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Glutathione is produced in the liver, but also may be taken as an antioxidant supplement.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Oral products exist, but absorption, formulation and clinical benefit vary."},{"q":"Evening primrose oil is a good source of omega-3 fatty acids.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Evening primrose oil is mainly an omega-6 source, including gamma-linolenic acid."}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":6,"title":"Phytotherapy, Homeopathy and Aromatherapy","kicker":"Learn the source’s botanical, essential-oil and homeopathic frameworks while keeping identity, dose, evidence and safety clearly separated.","steps":[{"id":"nhf6-1","chapter":"Botanical modalities and safety","chapterSummary":null,"title":"Botanical medicine and safety guardrails","highYield":"The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy.","teach":["The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy. A plant can have multiple common names but one botanical name; correct identification includes genus, species and relevant plant part. Natural does not mean harmless: herbs can cause adverse effects, allergy, toxicity and drug interactions.","Clarify whether the learner is studying historical use, current evidence or safe practice—these are different questions. Pregnancy, childhood, serious illness, surgery, polypharmacy and persistent symptoms are coordination or referral triggers."],"keyPoints":["A plant can have multiple common names but one botanical name; correct identification includes genus, species and relevant plant part.","Natural does not mean harmless: herbs can cause adverse effects, allergy, toxicity and drug interactions.","Clarify whether the learner is studying historical use, current evidence or safe practice—these are different questions."],"visual":{"title":"Botanical medicine and safety guardrails · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"A plant can have multiple common names but one botanical name; correct identification includes genus, species and relevant plant part."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Natural does not mean harmless: herbs can cause adverse effects, allergy, toxicity and drug interactions."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Clarify whether the learner is studying historical use, current evidence or safe practice—these are different questions."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BMSG","explanation":"Use BMSG to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The PDF groups modalities with very different evidence bases. Keep herbal pharmacology, aromatherapy and homeopathy distinct.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Plant identity, forms and preparation","highYield":"Preparation changes exposure: tea, infusion, decoction, tincture, extract, juice, bath, poultice and essential oil are not interchangeable.","teach":["Preparation changes exposure: tea, infusion, decoction, tincture, extract, juice, bath, poultice and essential oil are not interchangeable. Infusions suit many leaves and flowers; decoctions use longer simmering for tougher roots or bark; tinctures use alcohol or glycerin extraction. Record botanical name, plant part, extraction ratio or standardization, dose, route and duration.","The source proposes whole-plant synergy; treat this as a hypothesis or traditional rationale, not proof that whole herbs are always safer. Use product-specific instructions and avoid casual internal use of concentrated essential oils."],"keyPoints":["Infusions suit many leaves and flowers; decoctions use longer simmering for tougher roots or bark; tinctures use alcohol or glycerin extraction.","Record botanical name, plant part, extraction ratio or standardization, dose, route and duration.","The source proposes whole-plant synergy; treat this as a hypothesis or traditional rationale, not proof that whole herbs are always safer."],"visual":{"title":"Plant identity, forms and preparation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Preparation changes exposure: tea, infusion, decoction, tincture, extract, juice, bath, poultice and essential oil are not interchangeable."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Infusions suit many leaves and flowers; decoctions use longer simmering for tougher roots or bark; tinctures use alcohol or glycerin extraction."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Record botanical name, plant part, extraction ratio or standardization, dose, route and duration."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The source proposes whole-plant synergy; treat this as a hypothesis or traditional rationale, not proof that whole herbs are always safer."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"PIFP","explanation":"Use PIFP to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Traditional preparation guidance does not replace quality testing, contamination control, dosing data or clinical evidence.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Herb atlas I: digestion, respiration and immunity","highYield":"Digestive-source associations include angelica, cinnamon or yellow gentian for appetite, fennel or peppermint for cramping and marsh mallow for soothing mucosa.","teach":["Digestive-source associations include angelica, cinnamon or yellow gentian for appetite, fennel or peppermint for cramping and marsh mallow for soothing mucosa. Respiratory-source associations include thyme, elder flower, coltsfoot, eucalyptus and echinacea. Agrimony is linked with mild acute diarrhea; dandelion or artichoke with bile and digestion; black walnut with parasites in the source.","Compare claimed action, preparation and safety rather than memorizing an herb as a cure-all. Severe pain, dehydration, bleeding, breathing difficulty, persistent fever or prolonged symptoms need medical assessment."],"keyPoints":["Respiratory-source associations include thyme, elder flower, coltsfoot, eucalyptus and echinacea.","Agrimony is linked with mild acute diarrhea; dandelion or artichoke with bile and digestion; black walnut with parasites in the source.","Compare claimed action, preparation and safety rather than memorizing an herb as a cure-all."],"visual":{"title":"Herb atlas I: digestion, respiration and immunity · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Digestive-source associations include angelica, cinnamon or yellow gentian for appetite, fennel or peppermint for cramping and marsh mallow for soothing mucosa."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Respiratory-source associations include thyme, elder flower, coltsfoot, eucalyptus and echinacea."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Agrimony is linked with mild acute diarrhea; dandelion or artichoke with bile and digestion; black walnut with parasites in the source."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Compare claimed action, preparation and safety rather than memorizing an herb as a cure-all."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"HADRI","explanation":"Use HADRI to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Many indications are traditional, broad or unsupported; coltsfoot and some other products can pose toxicity concerns.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Herb atlas II: circulation, sleep, skin, pain and hormones","highYield":"Circulation-source associations include cayenne, ginkgo, hawthorn and horse chestnut; each has different evidence and interaction risks.","teach":["Circulation-source associations include cayenne, ginkgo, hawthorn and horse chestnut; each has different evidence and interaction risks. Sleep or calming associations include hops, lavender, lemon balm, passion flower and valerian. Skin or injury associations include aloe, calendula, arnica and witch hazel; route matters, and ordinary arnica herb is not used internally.","Pain or inflammation associations include white willow, feverfew, arnica and devil’s claw. Hormone-related associations include black cohosh, chasteberry and wild yam; screen carefully rather than promising hormone balance."],"keyPoints":["Sleep or calming associations include hops, lavender, lemon balm, passion flower and valerian.","Skin or injury associations include aloe, calendula, arnica and witch hazel; route matters, and ordinary arnica herb is not used internally.","Pain or inflammation associations include white willow, feverfew, arnica and devil’s claw."],"visual":{"title":"Herb atlas II: circulation, sleep, skin, pain and hormones · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Circulation-source associations include cayenne, ginkgo, hawthorn and horse chestnut; each has different evidence and interaction risks."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Sleep or calming associations include hops, lavender, lemon balm, passion flower and valerian."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Skin or injury associations include aloe, calendula, arnica and witch hazel; route matters, and ordinary arnica herb is not used internally."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Pain or inflammation associations include white willow, feverfew, arnica and devil’s claw."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"HACSS","explanation":"Use HACSS to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The source overstates dementia prevention, hormone effects and disease treatment. Ginkgo, willow, St John’s wort and many others can interact with medicines.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Essential oils: extraction, carriers, dilution and notes","highYield":"Essential or volatile oils are concentrated plant extracts, commonly produced by distillation; fragrance oils are not the same product.","teach":["Essential or volatile oils are concentrated plant extracts, commonly produced by distillation; fragrance oils are not the same product. Terpenes, terpene alcohols and aromatic compounds contribute scent and laboratory activity. Top notes evaporate quickly; middle notes are warm and mellow; base notes are deeper and longer lasting.","Carrier oils dilute essential oils for topical use; the source lists sweet almond, grapeseed, soybean and wheat germ. The source basic kit is camomile, eucalyptus, lavender and tea tree—exam associations, not universal treatments."],"keyPoints":["Terpenes, terpene alcohols and aromatic compounds contribute scent and laboratory activity.","Top notes evaporate quickly; middle notes are warm and mellow; base notes are deeper and longer lasting.","Carrier oils dilute essential oils for topical use; the source lists sweet almond, grapeseed, soybean and wheat germ."],"visual":{"title":"Essential oils: extraction, carriers, dilution and notes · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Essential or volatile oils are concentrated plant extracts, commonly produced by distillation; fragrance oils are not the same product."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Terpenes, terpene alcohols and aromatic compounds contribute scent and laboratory activity."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Top notes evaporate quickly; middle notes are warm and mellow; base notes are deeper and longer lasting."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Carrier oils dilute essential oils for topical use; the source lists sweet almond, grapeseed, soybean and wheat germ."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"EOECD","explanation":"Use EOECD to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Concentration and route drive risk. Avoid casual ingestion, use conservative dilution and consider pets, children, pregnancy, asthma and allergy.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Aromatherapy practice and seasonal-allergy safety","highYield":"Seasonal allergic rhinitis can involve sneezing, congestion, runny nose, itchy or watery eyes and fatigue; asthma symptoms are a red flag.","teach":["Seasonal allergic rhinitis can involve sneezing, congestion, runny nose, itchy or watery eyes and fatigue; asthma symptoms are a red flag. The article associates camomile with eye discomfort, frankincense with congestion, and thyme or eucalyptus with respiratory or immune claims. Aromatherapy delivery includes inhalation, diffusion, bathing, compresses and diluted massage.","Start with symptom severity, asthma history, medicines, pregnancy, skin sensitivity and prior allergy—not with an oil recipe. Use evidence-based allergy management alongside any optional scent-based comfort practice."],"keyPoints":["The article associates camomile with eye discomfort, frankincense with congestion, and thyme or eucalyptus with respiratory or immune claims.","Aromatherapy delivery includes inhalation, diffusion, bathing, compresses and diluted massage.","Start with symptom severity, asthma history, medicines, pregnancy, skin sensitivity and prior allergy—not with an oil recipe."],"visual":{"title":"Aromatherapy practice and seasonal-allergy safety · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Seasonal allergic rhinitis can involve sneezing, congestion, runny nose, itchy or watery eyes and fatigue; asthma symptoms are a red flag."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The article associates camomile with eye discomfort, frankincense with congestion, and thyme or eucalyptus with respiratory or immune claims."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Aromatherapy delivery includes inhalation, diffusion, bathing, compresses and diluted massage."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Start with symptom severity, asthma history, medicines, pregnancy, skin sensitivity and prior allergy—not with an oil recipe."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"APSS","explanation":"Use APSS to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not reproduce eye-area spray or direct-under-nose oil instructions unqualified. Essential oils can irritate eyes, airways and skin.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Botanical oils and balms","highYield":"The article profiles coconut, sea-buckthorn, argan, soybean, jojoba, avocado, sesame and olive oils for culinary or topical use.","teach":["The article profiles coconut, sea-buckthorn, argan, soybean, jojoba, avocado, sesame and olive oils for culinary or topical use. Match texture and formulation to the intended use rather than assuming one oil suits every skin type. Cold-pressed, expeller-pressed and refined oils differ in processing and composition; extraction method alone does not prove clinical superiority.","Jojoba is a liquid wax; argan and olive contain unsaturated fats or tocopherols; avocado is a heavier emollient; coconut is more occlusive. Check allergy history—especially sesame or nut concerns—and patch test a finished product when appropriate."],"keyPoints":["Match texture and formulation to the intended use rather than assuming one oil suits every skin type.","Cold-pressed, expeller-pressed and refined oils differ in processing and composition; extraction method alone does not prove clinical superiority.","Jojoba is a liquid wax; argan and olive contain unsaturated fats or tocopherols; avocado is a heavier emollient; coconut is more occlusive."],"visual":{"title":"Botanical oils and balms · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The article profiles coconut, sea-buckthorn, argan, soybean, jojoba, avocado, sesame and olive oils for culinary or topical use."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Match texture and formulation to the intended use rather than assuming one oil suits every skin type."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Cold-pressed, expeller-pressed and refined oils differ in processing and composition; extraction method alone does not prove clinical superiority."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Jojoba is a liquid wax; argan and olive contain unsaturated fats or tocopherols; avocado is a heavier emollient; coconut is more occlusive."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BOB","explanation":"Use BOB to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Anti-aging, collagen, wound-healing and antimicrobial claims are preliminary or formulation-specific. Cosmetic use is not infection treatment.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Homeopathy: theory, history, dilution and evidence","highYield":"Hahnemann’s homeopathic framework uses the law of similars: a substance associated with symptoms is selected for similar symptoms.","teach":["Hahnemann’s homeopathic framework uses the law of similars: a substance associated with symptoms is selected for similar symptoms. Remedies are serially diluted and shaken; X denotes 1:10 steps and C denotes 1:100 steps. The source minimum-dose doctrine claims more dilution can mean greater potency.","Classical homeopathy typically chooses one individualized remedy; complex products combine several remedies for a named concern. Learn the framework as course history and theory, then explicitly compare it with current evidence."],"keyPoints":["Remedies are serially diluted and shaken; X denotes 1:10 steps and C denotes 1:100 steps.","The source minimum-dose doctrine claims more dilution can mean greater potency.","Classical homeopathy typically chooses one individualized remedy; complex products combine several remedies for a named concern."],"visual":{"title":"Homeopathy: theory, history, dilution and evidence · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Hahnemann’s homeopathic framework uses the law of similars: a substance associated with symptoms is selected for similar symptoms."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Remedies are serially diluted and shaken; X denotes 1:10 steps and C denotes 1:100 steps."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source minimum-dose doctrine claims more dilution can mean greater potency."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Classical homeopathy typically chooses one individualized remedy; complex products combine several remedies for a named concern."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"HTHDE","explanation":"Use HTHDE to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Highly diluted homeopathic products have not demonstrated effects beyond placebo for treating conditions. Never delay effective care or stop medicines.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Homeopathic remedy recognition for the exam","highYield":"Recognize source pairs: arnica with bruising, overexertion or jet lag; Rhus toxicodendron with itchy rash or chickenpox; Nux vomica with indigestion or hangover; Ipecacuanha with persistent nausea.","teach":["Recognize source pairs: arnica with bruising, overexertion or jet lag; Rhus toxicodendron with itchy rash or chickenpox; Nux vomica with indigestion or hangover; Ipecacuanha with persistent nausea. Additional pairs include Aconite with early infection or anxiety, Cantharis with burning urinary symptoms, Magnesia phosphorica with cramps and Veratrum album with acute watery diarrhea. Distinguish a homeopathic product name from an herbal preparation of the same plant; concentration and risk can be entirely different.","Use retrieval to match names to source descriptions, not to self-prescribe. Attach a red-flag or referral cue to every symptom cluster."],"keyPoints":["Additional pairs include Aconite with early infection or anxiety, Cantharis with burning urinary symptoms, Magnesia phosphorica with cramps and Veratrum album with acute watery diarrhea.","Distinguish a homeopathic product name from an herbal preparation of the same plant; concentration and risk can be entirely different.","Use retrieval to match names to source descriptions, not to self-prescribe."],"visual":{"title":"Homeopathic remedy recognition for the exam · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Recognize source pairs: arnica with bruising, overexertion or jet lag; Rhus toxicodendron with itchy rash or chickenpox; Nux vomica with indigestion or hangover; Ipecacuanha with persistent nausea."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Additional pairs include Aconite with early infection or anxiety, Cantharis with burning urinary symptoms, Magnesia phosphorica with cramps and Veratrum album with acute watery diarrhea."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Distinguish a homeopathic product name from an herbal preparation of the same plant; concentration and risk can be entirely different."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Use retrieval to match names to source descriptions, not to self-prescribe."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"HRRE","explanation":"Use HRRE to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"These are course associations, not validated recommendations. Chickenpox, dehydration, urinary symptoms, head injury and severe pain can require care.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Whole-person botanical toolkit","highYield":"The article organizes source remedies around hair, skin, nails, brain, women’s symptoms, muscles, heart, digestion, eyes, oral health, joints and feet.","teach":["The article organizes source remedies around hair, skin, nails, brain, women’s symptoms, muscles, heart, digestion, eyes, oral health, joints and feet. Begin each system with foundations: adequate food, sleep, movement, hygiene, stress support and indicated medical or dental care. Then classify any herb, oil or supplement by intended outcome, evidence, route, dose, safety and monitoring.","The glossary distinguishes teas, infusions, decoctions, tinctures, poultices, essential oils and distillates. Practise responding to a request with one clarification, one evidence boundary and one safe next step."],"keyPoints":["Begin each system with foundations: adequate food, sleep, movement, hygiene, stress support and indicated medical or dental care.","Then classify any herb, oil or supplement by intended outcome, evidence, route, dose, safety and monitoring.","The glossary distinguishes teas, infusions, decoctions, tinctures, poultices, essential oils and distillates."],"visual":{"title":"Whole-person botanical toolkit · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The article organizes source remedies around hair, skin, nails, brain, women’s symptoms, muscles, heart, digestion, eyes, oral health, joints and feet."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Begin each system with foundations: adequate food, sleep, movement, hygiene, stress support and indicated medical or dental care."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Then classify any herb, oil or supplement by intended outcome, evidence, route, dose, safety and monitoring."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The glossary distinguishes teas, infusions, decoctions, tinctures, poultices, essential oils and distillates."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"WBT","explanation":"Use WBT to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The article makes many treatment claims and uses vague detox language. Preserve it as course content without turning it into unqualified advice.","act":null},{"id":"nhf6-11","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 6 test and remediation","highYield":"Complete all 25 official items and tag whether each tests an herb, essential oil, aromatherapy concept or homeopathic remedy.","teach":["Complete all 25 official items and tag whether each tests an herb, essential oil, aromatherapy concept or homeopathic remedy. Answer with the source association before reading the evidence note. For every homeopathy item, label it course doctrine rather than established mechanism.","Review misses by rebuilding a small name-to-category-to-source-use-to-safety table. Retest low-confidence items and explain which answers should never become self-treatment advice."],"keyPoints":["Answer with the source association before reading the evidence note.","For every homeopathy item, label it course doctrine rather than established mechanism.","Review misses by rebuilding a small name-to-category-to-source-use-to-safety table."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 6 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Complete all 25 official items and tag whether each tests an herb, essential oil, aromatherapy concept or homeopathic remedy."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Answer with the source association before reading the evidence note."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"For every homeopathy item, label it course doctrine rather than established mechanism."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Review misses by rebuilding a small name-to-category-to-source-use-to-safety table."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then explain the evidence boundary."},"goodToKnow":"No official key was supplied; keys are inferred from the lesson. Question 15 duplicates an option label in the PDF; the option order is preserved.","act":null},{"id":"botanical-medicine-match","title":"Match it: Botanical medicine and safety guardrails · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy.","A plant can have multiple common names but one botanical name; correct identification includes genus, species and relevant plant part.","Natural does not mean harmless: herbs can cause adverse effects, allergy, toxicity and drug interactions.","Clarify whether the learner is studying historical use, current evidence or safe practice—these are different questions."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Clarify whether the learner is studying historical use, current evidence or safe practice—these are different questions."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: Natural does not mean harmless: herbs can cause adverse effects, allergy, toxicity and drug interactions."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: A plant can have multiple common names but one botanical name; correct identification includes genus, species and relevant plant part."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Botanical medicine and safety guardrails","back":"The lesson uses botanical medicine or phytotherapy as an umbrella introducing herbal medicine, aromatherapy and homeopathy."},{"front":"Plant identity, forms and preparation","back":"Preparation changes exposure: tea, infusion, decoction, tincture, extract, juice, bath, poultice and essential oil are not interchangeable."},{"front":"Herb atlas I: digestion, respiration and immunity","back":"Digestive-source associations include angelica, cinnamon or yellow gentian for appetite, fennel or peppermint for cramping and marsh mallow for soothing mucosa."},{"front":"Herb atlas II: circulation, sleep, skin, pain and hormones","back":"Circulation-source associations include cayenne, ginkgo, hawthorn and horse chestnut; each has different evidence and interaction risks."},{"front":"Essential oils: extraction, carriers, dilution and notes","back":"Essential or volatile oils are concentrated plant extracts, commonly produced by distillation; fragrance oils are not the same product."},{"front":"Aromatherapy practice and seasonal-allergy safety","back":"Seasonal allergic rhinitis can involve sneezing, congestion, runny nose, itchy or watery eyes and fatigue; asthma symptoms are a red flag."},{"front":"Botanical oils and balms","back":"The article profiles coconut, sea-buckthorn, argan, soybean, jojoba, avocado, sesame and olive oils for culinary or topical use."},{"front":"Homeopathy: theory, history, dilution and evidence","back":"Hahnemann’s homeopathic framework uses the law of similars: a substance associated with symptoms is selected for similar symptoms."},{"front":"Homeopathic remedy recognition for the exam","back":"Recognize source pairs: arnica with bruising, overexertion or jet lag; Rhus toxicodendron with itchy rash or chickenpox; Nux vomica with indigestion or hangover; Ipecacuanha with persistent nausea."},{"front":"Whole-person botanical toolkit","back":"The article organizes source remedies around hair, skin, nails, brain, women’s symptoms, muscles, heart, digestion, eyes, oral health, joints and feet."},{"front":"Official Lesson 6 test and remediation","back":"Complete all 25 official items and tag whether each tests an herb, essential oil, aromatherapy concept or homeopathic remedy."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of the following herbs may be recommended to someone with gastrointestinal disturbances?","o":["feverfew","fennel","ginkgo","black walnut","red clover"],"a":1,"e":"Fennel may help selected functional symptoms, but “gastrointestinal disturbances” is too broad for a recommendation."},{"q":"Devil’s claw is commonly used to:","o":["increase alertness","treat indigestion","treat musculoskeletal degenerative disorders","all of the above","Both B and C"],"a":4,"e":"Course/traditional-use answer; evidence and safety vary by condition and preparation."},{"q":"Which of the following herbs may be helpful in stimulating/enhancing the appetite?","o":["angelica root","cinnamon","devil’s claw","all of the above","A and B only"],"a":3,"e":"Traditional-use answer; appetite loss can be clinically significant and should not automatically be self-treated."},{"q":"Your friend Jason has terrible insomnia. What herbs might help him sleep?","o":["goldenseal","hops","peppermint","valerian","Either B or D"],"a":4,"e":"Evidence for valerian and hops is mixed; persistent insomnia warrants assessment and interaction screening."},{"q":"Commonly prescribed in Europe to treat symptoms of depression and anxiety, ____________________ is also used externally to treat burns.","o":["witch hazel","St. John’s wort","aloe vera","echinacea","black cohosh root"],"a":1,"e":"St John’s wort has important drug interactions and is not a general anxiety or burn self-treatment."},{"q":"Which of the following herbs can be used to address inflammations?","o":["white willow bark","camomile","arnica flower","uva ursi","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Course grouping is broad. Indication, route, preparation and safety differ substantially."},{"q":"Essential oils are used:","o":["as antiseptics","for aromatherapy","for their anti-inflammatory properties","all of the above","Both B and C"],"a":3,"e":"This describes uses and claims, not universal clinical efficacy. Oils can irritate, sensitize or be toxic if misused."},{"q":"Base notes in aromatherapy could include:","o":["patchouli, myrrh, and frankincense","grape seed, sweet almond, and wheat germ","lavender, tea tree, and cinnamon","bergamot, rosewood, and thyme","none of the above"],"a":0,"e":"This is an aroma classification, not evidence of medical efficacy."},{"q":"Stephen would like to find a homeopathic remedy for jet lag before leaving on his international flight. What could he try?","o":["bryonia","gelsemium","arnica","aconite","veratrum album"],"a":2,"e":"Course-doctrine answer only. Homeopathic arnica has not been shown to treat jet lag beyond placebo."},{"q":"Middle notes have a warm, mellow aroma and include:","o":["cinnamon","cedarwood","myrrh","rosewood","bergamot"],"a":3,"e":"Aroma classification only."},{"q":"________________________ is a common homeopathic remedy for chickenpox.","o":["magnesia phosphorica","phosphorus","cantharis","rhus toxicodendron","sepia officinalis"],"a":3,"e":"Course-doctrine answer only. Homeopathy is not an evidence-based chickenpox treatment."},{"q":"Aromatherapy uses many different oils including:","o":["terpene","goldenseal oils","saw palmetto","plantain alcohols","hawthorn alcohols"],"a":0,"e":"The source describes terpenes as constituents; terpene is a compound class rather than an oil name."},{"q":"The three classifications of oils do not include:","o":["base notes","treble notes","top notes","middle notes","all of the above"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Diana is excited about visiting an aromatherapist for the first time. During the diagnostic interview she might be asked questions about her EXCEPT:","o":["lifestyle","resting heart rate","background","health","none of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Inferred from the source list; this is not a universal scope-of-practice rule."},{"q":"A basic essential oil kit should include:","o":["tea tree","myrrh","coltsfoot","patchouli","bergamot"],"a":0,"e":"The PDF duplicates an option label. Tea tree is one of the source’s basic oils; essential oils require dilution and safety screening."},{"q":"The aloe vera plant is helpful in treating damaged tissue.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Some topical preparations may help selected minor skin conditions; oral aloe latex has important risks."},{"q":"Cayenne is useful in speeding up a sluggish pulse.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The source says cayenne supports circulation without speeding pulse."},{"q":"Acute diarrhea may be effectively treated with silica.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Homeopathic silica is not an evidence-based acute-diarrhea treatment; hydration and red-flag assessment matter."},{"q":"Echinacea can be used externally to help heal wounds.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Traditional-use course answer; clinical evidence and product quality are limited, and topical allergy is possible."},{"q":"Plants containing essential oils may have the ability to stimulate the immune system.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Laboratory immune effects do not establish clinically meaningful immune boosting in people."},{"q":"Prescription drugs may weaken the effects of a homeopathic remedy.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course-homeopathy doctrine, not an evidence-based interaction. Do not stop prescriptions to preserve a supposed remedy effect."},{"q":"Gingko is commonly recommended for its role in preventing memory loss and is also used to increase blood circulation to the brain.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer preserves the source spelling. Ginkgo is not established to prevent dementia and can increase bleeding risk."},{"q":"Feverfew has been used to relieve migraine headaches.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Feverfew has been studied more for prevention than acute relief; evidence is mixed."},{"q":"Ylang-ylang promotes restlessness.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The source classifies it as calming; clinical evidence is limited and scent responses vary."},{"q":"When used as an essential oil, lavender is known to have antiseptic qualities.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Laboratory antimicrobial activity does not make lavender a replacement for wound care or infection treatment."}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":7,"title":"A Healthy Lifestyle","kicker":"Connect movement, stress, sleep and environmental health while learning to question dated exposure and product claims.","steps":[{"id":"nhf7-1","chapter":"Lifestyle and environmental health","chapterSummary":null,"title":"Move more, feel better","highYield":"The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week.","teach":["The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week. Regular activity is associated in the lesson with lower blood pressure and cholesterol, stronger muscles and bones, improved mood and sleep, and reduced chronic-disease risk.","Choose activities that remain interesting, vary the type of movement and set measurable, realistic goals. Warm up, cool down and treat pain, dizziness or weakness as signals to stop or reduce intensity."],"keyPoints":["Regular activity is associated in the lesson with lower blood pressure and cholesterol, stronger muscles and bones, improved mood and sleep, and reduced chronic-disease risk.","Choose activities that remain interesting, vary the type of movement and set measurable, realistic goals.","Warm up, cool down and treat pain, dizziness or weakness as signals to stop or reduce intensity."],"visual":{"title":"Move more, feel better · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Regular activity is associated in the lesson with lower blood pressure and cholesterol, stronger muscles and bones, improved mood and sleep, and reduced chronic-disease risk."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Choose activities that remain interesting, vary the type of movement and set measurable, realistic goals."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Warm up, cool down and treat pain, dizziness or weakness as signals to stop or reduce intensity."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"MMFB","explanation":"Use MMFB to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Activity guidance has evolved; use current Canadian guidance for practice while remembering the source target for its test.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Stress: notice, reframe, recover","highYield":"The lesson frames coping as partly shaped by whether a challenge is interpreted as manageable or defeating.","teach":["The lesson frames coping as partly shaped by whether a challenge is interpreted as manageable or defeating. Workplace contributors can include poor air quality, insufficient natural light, noise and crowding.","Begin stress management by identifying environmental and behavioural contributors rather than treating stress as personal failure. When a stressor cannot be removed, recognize its effect and develop ways to counter it."],"keyPoints":["Workplace contributors can include poor air quality, insufficient natural light, noise and crowding.","Begin stress management by identifying environmental and behavioural contributors rather than treating stress as personal failure.","When a stressor cannot be removed, recognize its effect and develop ways to counter it."],"visual":{"title":"Stress: notice, reframe, recover · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson frames coping as partly shaped by whether a challenge is interpreted as manageable or defeating."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Workplace contributors can include poor air quality, insufficient natural light, noise and crowding."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Begin stress management by identifying environmental and behavioural contributors rather than treating stress as personal failure."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"When a stressor cannot be removed, recognize its effect and develop ways to counter it."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"SNRR","explanation":"Use SNRR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Stress responses are not purely a mindset problem; workload, trauma, illness, discrimination and resources matter.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Sleep is active repair","highYield":"The source recommends about seven and a half to nine hours of sleep for most healthy adults.","teach":["The source recommends about seven and a half to nine hours of sleep for most healthy adults. Sleep supports biological maintenance; chronic sleep loss can impair judgment, coordination and reaction time.","Use a dark, cool, ventilated and quiet room, maintain regular sleep and wake times, and avoid late caffeine, alcohol, heavy food and vigorous exercise. Deep breathing, a warm bath, regular daytime activity and a consistent pre-sleep routine are presented as supports."],"keyPoints":["Sleep supports biological maintenance; chronic sleep loss can impair judgment, coordination and reaction time.","Use a dark, cool, ventilated and quiet room, maintain regular sleep and wake times, and avoid late caffeine, alcohol, heavy food and vigorous exercise.","Deep breathing, a warm bath, regular daytime activity and a consistent pre-sleep routine are presented as supports."],"visual":{"title":"Sleep is active repair · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The source recommends about seven and a half to nine hours of sleep for most healthy adults."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Sleep supports biological maintenance; chronic sleep loss can impair judgment, coordination and reaction time."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Use a dark, cool, ventilated and quiet room, maintain regular sleep and wake times, and avoid late caffeine, alcohol, heavy food and vigorous exercise."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Deep breathing, a warm bath, regular daytime activity and a consistent pre-sleep routine are presented as supports."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"SAR","explanation":"Use SAR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Sleep need varies and persistent insomnia, loud snoring, breathing pauses or severe daytime sleepiness need assessment.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Build your exposure map","highYield":"The lesson links environmental exposure with symptoms such as fatigue, headache, allergies, respiratory complaints, eye irritation and nausea.","teach":["The lesson links environmental exposure with symptoms such as fatigue, headache, allergies, respiratory complaints, eye irritation and nausea. Possible sources span home, office, outdoor environment, cleaning products, personal-care products, combustion, furnishings and building materials.","Symptoms are nonspecific; noticing a pattern does not by itself identify a cause. Persistent or serious symptoms require appropriate medical and environmental assessment."],"keyPoints":["Possible sources span home, office, outdoor environment, cleaning products, personal-care products, combustion, furnishings and building materials.","Symptoms are nonspecific; noticing a pattern does not by itself identify a cause.","Persistent or serious symptoms require appropriate medical and environmental assessment."],"visual":{"title":"Build your exposure map · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson links environmental exposure with symptoms such as fatigue, headache, allergies, respiratory complaints, eye irritation and nausea."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Possible sources span home, office, outdoor environment, cleaning products, personal-care products, combustion, furnishings and building materials."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Symptoms are nonspecific; noticing a pattern does not by itself identify a cause."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Persistent or serious symptoms require appropriate medical and environmental assessment."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BYE","explanation":"Use BYE to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The source uses broad chemical-sensitivity language. A temporal pattern can guide investigation but does not prove causation.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Create a healthier home and office","highYield":"Named indoor contaminants include tobacco smoke, gas appliances, formaldehyde-emitting board products, synthetic furnishings, dust, mould and bacteria.","teach":["Named indoor contaminants include tobacco smoke, gas appliances, formaldehyde-emitting board products, synthetic furnishings, dust, mould and bacteria. Ventilation, source control, appropriate cleaning, daylight and attention to ergonomics are practical first steps.","The source presents selected houseplants and negative-ion generators as air-quality aids. Room-scale plant effects are limited, and some ionizers can generate ozone."],"keyPoints":["Ventilation, source control, appropriate cleaning, daylight and attention to ergonomics are practical first steps.","The source presents selected houseplants and negative-ion generators as air-quality aids.","Room-scale plant effects are limited, and some ionizers can generate ozone."],"visual":{"title":"Create a healthier home and office · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Named indoor contaminants include tobacco smoke, gas appliances, formaldehyde-emitting board products, synthetic furnishings, dust, mould and bacteria."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Ventilation, source control, appropriate cleaning, daylight and attention to ergonomics are practical first steps."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source presents selected houseplants and negative-ion generators as air-quality aids."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Room-scale plant effects are limited, and some ionizers can generate ozone."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"CHHO","explanation":"Use CHHO to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not use houseplants or ionizers as substitutes for fixing moisture, combustion or ventilation problems.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Air, water, light and EMFs","highYield":"The course says inexpensive pitcher filters generally target chlorine and lead, while most home filters do not remove E. coli unless specifically designed to do so.","teach":["The course says inexpensive pitcher filters generally target chlorine and lead, while most home filters do not remove E. coli unless specifically designed to do so. Filter selection should be based on the contaminant, device specifications, certification and timely cartridge replacement. Daytime outdoor light and appropriately timed bright-light therapy are relevant to seasonal affective symptoms.","The source gives technically unreliable EMF frequency ranges; retain them only for source-test recognition, not practice. Bottled-water production is presented as having substantial plastic, energy and waste impacts."],"keyPoints":["Filter selection should be based on the contaminant, device specifications, certification and timely cartridge replacement.","Daytime outdoor light and appropriately timed bright-light therapy are relevant to seasonal affective symptoms.","The source gives technically unreliable EMF frequency ranges; retain them only for source-test recognition, not practice."],"visual":{"title":"Air, water, light and EMFs · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The course says inexpensive pitcher filters generally target chlorine and lead, while most home filters do not remove E. coli unless specifically designed to do so."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Filter selection should be based on the contaminant, device specifications, certification and timely cartridge replacement."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Daytime outdoor light and appropriately timed bright-light therapy are relevant to seasonal affective symptoms."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The source gives technically unreliable EMF frequency ranges; retain them only for source-test recognition, not practice."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"AWLE","explanation":"Use AWLE to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"No filter removes every contaminant. Verify local water reports and product-specific certified reduction claims. EMF health claims in the source are weak.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Read the label, not the promise","highYield":"The lesson says the word natural on personal-care labels does not guarantee safety or organic production.","teach":["The lesson says the word natural on personal-care labels does not guarantee safety or organic production. Its source guide distinguishes 100 percent organic, certified organic and made-with-organic-ingredients thresholds.","The cosmetic article challenges myths about absorption, vegan labels, price, ingredient disclosure and legal availability. Risk depends on the specific ingredient, dose, route, exposure and person; an ingredient list is not a blanket toxicity verdict."],"keyPoints":["Its source guide distinguishes 100 percent organic, certified organic and made-with-organic-ingredients thresholds.","The cosmetic article challenges myths about absorption, vegan labels, price, ingredient disclosure and legal availability.","Risk depends on the specific ingredient, dose, route, exposure and person; an ingredient list is not a blanket toxicity verdict."],"visual":{"title":"Read the label, not the promise · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson says the word natural on personal-care labels does not guarantee safety or organic production."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Its source guide distinguishes 100 percent organic, certified organic and made-with-organic-ingredients thresholds."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The cosmetic article challenges myths about absorption, vegan labels, price, ingredient disclosure and legal availability."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Risk depends on the specific ingredient, dose, route, exposure and person; an ingredient list is not a blanket toxicity verdict."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"RLP","explanation":"Use RLP to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Organic label rules depend on product category and jurisdiction. Several ingredient claims in the source use hazard language without realistic exposure context.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Work smarter: standing and postural rotation","highYield":"The article associates prolonged uninterrupted sitting with poorer metabolic and cardiovascular outcomes.","teach":["The article associates prolonged uninterrupted sitting with poorer metabolic and cardiovascular outcomes. A standing desk is not an instruction to stand all day; alternate sitting, standing and movement.","Place the screen slightly below eye level and keep the arms near a 90-degree angle to the work surface. Ease into standing, use sitting breaks and consider an antifatigue mat."],"keyPoints":["A standing desk is not an instruction to stand all day; alternate sitting, standing and movement.","Place the screen slightly below eye level and keep the arms near a 90-degree angle to the work surface.","Ease into standing, use sitting breaks and consider an antifatigue mat."],"visual":{"title":"Work smarter: standing and postural rotation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The article associates prolonged uninterrupted sitting with poorer metabolic and cardiovascular outcomes."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"A standing desk is not an instruction to stand all day; alternate sitting, standing and movement."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Place the screen slightly below eye level and keep the arms near a 90-degree angle to the work surface."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Ease into standing, use sitting breaks and consider an antifatigue mat."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"WSSPR","explanation":"Use WSSPR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The key intervention is postural variety and movement, not one perfect workstation position.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Fast fashion and sick buildings","highYield":"Fast fashion is examined through environmental waste, pesticide and chemical use, worker exposure, working conditions and residues reaching consumers.","teach":["Fast fashion is examined through environmental waste, pesticide and chemical use, worker exposure, working conditions and residues reaching consumers. More durable garments, reduced consumption, reuse, repair and responsible production reduce the burden described. Sick building syndrome describes nonspecific symptoms associated with time in a poorly ventilated or contaminated building.","Possible contributors include VOCs, smoke, mould, bacteria, radon, asbestos, poor lighting, acoustics and ergonomics. Useful responses include documenting time-place symptom patterns, improving ventilation and source control, and involving qualified professionals."],"keyPoints":["More durable garments, reduced consumption, reuse, repair and responsible production reduce the burden described.","Sick building syndrome describes nonspecific symptoms associated with time in a poorly ventilated or contaminated building.","Possible contributors include VOCs, smoke, mould, bacteria, radon, asbestos, poor lighting, acoustics and ergonomics."],"visual":{"title":"Fast fashion and sick buildings · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Fast fashion is examined through environmental waste, pesticide and chemical use, worker exposure, working conditions and residues reaching consumers."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"More durable garments, reduced consumption, reuse, repair and responsible production reduce the burden described."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Sick building syndrome describes nonspecific symptoms associated with time in a poorly ventilated or contaminated building."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Possible contributors include VOCs, smoke, mould, bacteria, radon, asbestos, poor lighting, acoustics and ergonomics."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FFSB","explanation":"Use FFSB to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Symptoms alone cannot identify a building cause; emergencies such as carbon monoxide exposure require immediate response.","act":null},{"id":"nhf7-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 7 test and remediation","highYield":"Retrieve the course activity dose, exercise warning signs, workplace stressors, seasonal-light strategy and sleep supports.","teach":["Retrieve the course activity dose, exercise warning signs, workplace stressors, seasonal-light strategy and sleep supports. Distinguish inexpensive-filter claims from E. coli removal and recall the source organic-label thresholds.","Practise identifying indoor-air sources, household products, personal-care claims and the source EMF statements. Answer using the inferred source key, then review every dated or weak-evidence item separately before carrying it into practice."],"keyPoints":["Distinguish inexpensive-filter claims from E. coli removal and recall the source organic-label thresholds.","Practise identifying indoor-air sources, household products, personal-care claims and the source EMF statements.","Answer using the inferred source key, then review every dated or weak-evidence item separately before carrying it into practice."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 7 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Retrieve the course activity dose, exercise warning signs, workplace stressors, seasonal-light strategy and sleep supports."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Distinguish inexpensive-filter claims from E. coli removal and recall the source organic-label thresholds."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Practise identifying indoor-air sources, household products, personal-care claims and the source EMF statements."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Answer using the inferred source key, then review every dated or weak-evidence item separately before carrying it into practice."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The supplied printer test contains no official answer key; all keys are inferred from the lesson and visibly labelled.","act":null},{"id":"healthy-lifestyle-match","title":"Match it: Move more, feel better · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week.","Regular activity is associated in the lesson with lower blood pressure and cholesterol, stronger muscles and bones, improved mood and sleep, and reduced chronic-disease risk.","Choose activities that remain interesting, vary the type of movement and set measurable, realistic goals.","Warm up, cool down and treat pain, dizziness or weakness as signals to stop or reduce intensity."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Warm up, cool down and treat pain, dizziness or weakness as signals to stop or reduce intensity."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: Choose activities that remain interesting, vary the type of movement and set measurable, realistic goals."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: Regular activity is associated in the lesson with lower blood pressure and cholesterol, stronger muscles and bones, improved mood and sleep, and reduced chronic-disease risk."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Move more, feel better","back":"The course sets a historical minimum target of 20 minutes of exercise on three to four days each week."},{"front":"Stress: notice, reframe, recover","back":"The lesson frames coping as partly shaped by whether a challenge is interpreted as manageable or defeating."},{"front":"Sleep is active repair","back":"The source recommends about seven and a half to nine hours of sleep for most healthy adults."},{"front":"Build your exposure map","back":"The lesson links environmental exposure with symptoms such as fatigue, headache, allergies, respiratory complaints, eye irritation and nausea."},{"front":"Create a healthier home and office","back":"Named indoor contaminants include tobacco smoke, gas appliances, formaldehyde-emitting board products, synthetic furnishings, dust, mould and bacteria."},{"front":"Air, water, light and EMFs","back":"The course says inexpensive pitcher filters generally target chlorine and lead, while most home filters do not remove E. coli unless specifically designed to do so."},{"front":"Read the label, not the promise","back":"The lesson says the word natural on personal-care labels does not guarantee safety or organic production."},{"front":"Work smarter: standing and postural rotation","back":"The article associates prolonged uninterrupted sitting with poorer metabolic and cardiovascular outcomes."},{"front":"Fast fashion and sick buildings","back":"Fast fashion is examined through environmental waste, pesticide and chemical use, worker exposure, working conditions and residues reaching consumers."},{"front":"Official Lesson 7 test and remediation","back":"Retrieve the course activity dose, exercise warning signs, workplace stressors, seasonal-light strategy and sleep supports."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A healthy lifestyle should include a minimum of ___ minutes of exercise a day, _____ times a week.","o":["30 / five","30 / three to four","20 / two to three","20 / three to four","none of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Historical course target. Use current Canadian physical-activity guidance for real-world planning."},{"q":"A regular program of physical activity can:","o":["reduce blood pressure","reduce feelings of depression and anger","reduce risk for heart attack, cancer, diabetes, and stroke","all of the above","A and C above"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Dizziness or pain experienced while exercising are signals from your body that you are:","o":["working too hard","hitting the zone","burning calories","out of shape","achieving a good cardio rate"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following may be signs of chemical sensitivity?","o":["allergies","respiratory problems","eye inflammations","nausea","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"These symptoms are nonspecific and do not establish that a chemical exposure is the cause."},{"q":"Sarah is concerned about trying to do too many fitness activities at once. She should:","o":["only participate in one activity per season for maximum effectiveness","focus on ones that keep her interested","stick to brisk walking to be safe","only do activities where stretching and flexibility is involved","ensure that all activities can include a hydrotherapy component"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Electromagnetic fields to which we may be exposed range between _____ to _____ hertz (Hz) for domestic appliances and _____ to _____ Hz for high-voltage power lines.","o":["20 to 30 Hz / 500 to 600 Hz","50 to 60 Hz / 200 to 300 Hz","7 to 8 Hz / 20 to 30 Hz","120 to 130 Hz / 500 to 600 Hz","60 to 75 Hz / 200 to 300 Hz"],"a":1,"e":"Source answer retained for the test. The technical framing is dated and unreliable for current EMF assessment."},{"q":"Which of the following factors contribute to indoor air pollution?","o":["stain-proofing fabric treatments","particleboard","machines that emit negative ions","A and B above","all of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The source keys A and B. Some ionizers can also generate ozone, so product-specific evaluation matters."},{"q":"Most inexpensive store-bought water filters can remove:","o":["chlorine and E. coli","bacteria and lead","chlorine and lead","charcoal and bacteria","E. coli and heavy metals"],"a":2,"e":"Historical product generalization. Filter performance is contaminant- and certification-specific."},{"q":"Harmful household products include:","o":["laundry detergents","drain cleaners","pesticides","moth balls","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Shaughna has decided to embark on a new fitness program. She should:","o":["start with activities that work out only one muscle group","focus on aerobics or yoga","set measurable goals that are realistic","choose a new exercise every week","try to do the least strenuous activities for maximum effectiveness"],"a":2,"e":""},{"q":"Which of the following might contribute to stress in the workplace?","o":["insufficient natural lighting","crowding","poor air quality","All of the above","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Ways to alleviate seasonal affective disorder (SAD) symptoms include:","o":["using partial-spectrum lighting","removing halogen lights from most work areas","using fluorescent lights in large open areas","going outside during the day as much as possible","none of the above"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Sufficient and appropriate physical activity will help attain all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["muscle strength and endurance","reducing blood pressure","boosting metabolism","reducing depression","reducing bone density"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Not paying attention to toxins in the indoor and outdoor environment could cause some individuals to suffer from:","o":["fatigue and headaches","synthetic chemical disorder","EMF resistance","negative ion immunity","combustible emission syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Fatigue and headache are nonspecific; identify and measure a plausible exposure rather than assuming “toxins.”"},{"q":"Most home water filters are not designed to remove:","o":["chlorine","E. coli","lead","heavy metals","all of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Historical source answer. Check the exact filter’s certified contaminant-reduction claims."},{"q":"Seasonal affective disorder may be alleviated with the use of full-spectrum lighting or halogen lights indoors during dark winter months.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Bright-light therapy can help SAD when appropriately timed and dosed; ordinary halogen or “full-spectrum” labels are not interchangeable with a therapeutic light box."},{"q":"Harmful chemicals may be found in personal care products such as toothpaste, shampoo, and deodorants.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Hazard and risk depend on the specific ingredient, dose, route and exposure."},{"q":"Electromagnetic fields generated by household appliances, computers, and wiring are no greater than that of the earth’s magnetic field.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Source framing is weak; field strength and frequency vary, and strength alone does not establish a health effect."},{"q":"BHA, parabens, sodium laureth sulfate, and petrolatum are all toxic substances that can be found in a variety of personal care products.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer. This blanket toxic classification is not a sound current risk assessment; ingredient, dose and route matter."},{"q":"Products labeled as \"Made with Organic ingredients\" must contain at least 95% organic ingredients (excluding water and salt).","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Label thresholds are jurisdiction- and category-specific. The source distinguishes “made with” from 95% certified-organic claims."},{"q":"Exercise can lower cholesterol levels.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Physical exercise can help sleeplessness by improving circulation.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Exercise can support sleep, but improved circulation is not a complete or proven single mechanism."},{"q":"A great all-purpose cleaner can be made by combining equal amounts of water and vinegar.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Do not mix vinegar with bleach. Vinegar is unsuitable for some stone surfaces and is not a universal disinfectant."},{"q":"Indoor air quality has improved over the years to the point where it is now safer than outdoor air.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Some houseplants can help clear formaldehyde from the air.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True in chamber studies; ordinary room-scale effects are limited and plants do not replace source control or ventilation."}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":8,"title":"Physical, Mental, Emotional and Spiritual Health","kicker":"Explore mind–body practices, bodywork and self-regulation while separating comfort, theory and evidence-based clinical care.","steps":[{"id":"nhf8-1","chapter":"Mind–body medicine and safe application","chapterSummary":null,"title":"The mind–body loop","highYield":"The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system.","teach":["The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system. Mind–body medicine integrates physical, mental, emotional and spiritual dimensions in its model of health.","The lesson groups breathing, meditation, bodywork, biofeedback and cognitive-behavioural methods under this umbrella. Separate the broad mind–body principle from stronger claims that a specific practice treats a specific disease."],"keyPoints":["Mind–body medicine integrates physical, mental, emotional and spiritual dimensions in its model of health.","The lesson groups breathing, meditation, bodywork, biofeedback and cognitive-behavioural methods under this umbrella.","Separate the broad mind–body principle from stronger claims that a specific practice treats a specific disease."],"visual":{"title":"The mind–body loop · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Mind–body medicine integrates physical, mental, emotional and spiritual dimensions in its model of health."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The lesson groups breathing, meditation, bodywork, biofeedback and cognitive-behavioural methods under this umbrella."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Separate the broad mind–body principle from stronger claims that a specific practice treats a specific disease."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"ML","explanation":"Use ML to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Mind and body interact through multiple neural, endocrine, immune and behavioural pathways; one pathway is not a universal treatment mechanism.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Breathe to shift state","highYield":"Shallow chest breathing is associated in the lesson with fight-or-flight, while diaphragmatic breathing is presented as calming.","teach":["Shallow chest breathing is associated in the lesson with fight-or-flight, while diaphragmatic breathing is presented as calming. During relaxed breathing, the abdomen rises first while the chest rises more subtly.","The source teaches breathing awareness, paced deep breathing, alternate-nostril breathing and ocean breathing. Practise without strain and stop if exercises cause dizziness, distress or uncomfortable breathlessness."],"keyPoints":["During relaxed breathing, the abdomen rises first while the chest rises more subtly.","The source teaches breathing awareness, paced deep breathing, alternate-nostril breathing and ocean breathing.","Practise without strain and stop if exercises cause dizziness, distress or uncomfortable breathlessness."],"visual":{"title":"Breathe to shift state · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Shallow chest breathing is associated in the lesson with fight-or-flight, while diaphragmatic breathing is presented as calming."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"During relaxed breathing, the abdomen rises first while the chest rises more subtly."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source teaches breathing awareness, paced deep breathing, alternate-nostril breathing and ocean breathing."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Practise without strain and stop if exercises cause dizziness, distress or uncomfortable breathlessness."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BSS","explanation":"Use BSS to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Breathing changes can influence arousal, but “air filling the diaphragm” is imprecise—the diaphragm is a muscle and the lungs fill.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Meditation: train attention and physiology","highYield":"Meditation is quiet contemplation focused on breath, a phrase, a mantra, a theme or present-moment experience.","teach":["Meditation is quiet contemplation focused on breath, a phrase, a mantra, a theme or present-moment experience. The lesson places meditation in multiple religious traditions as well as secular practice. Proposed benefits include reduced stress, improved concentration, lower cardiovascular arousal and pain support.","The source calls meditation-related neuronal signals gamma waves; the test therefore keys gamma waves, not alpha waves. Immune, tumour, infection and accelerated-healing claims require evidence caveats."],"keyPoints":["The lesson places meditation in multiple religious traditions as well as secular practice.","Proposed benefits include reduced stress, improved concentration, lower cardiovascular arousal and pain support.","The source calls meditation-related neuronal signals gamma waves; the test therefore keys gamma waves, not alpha waves."],"visual":{"title":"Meditation: train attention and physiology · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Meditation is quiet contemplation focused on breath, a phrase, a mantra, a theme or present-moment experience."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The lesson places meditation in multiple religious traditions as well as secular practice."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Proposed benefits include reduced stress, improved concentration, lower cardiovascular arousal and pain support."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The source calls meditation-related neuronal signals gamma waves; the test therefore keys gamma waves, not alpha waves."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"MTAP","explanation":"Use MTAP to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Meditation can be helpful but may feel difficult or destabilizing for some people; offer choice and qualified support.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Bodywork map","highYield":"The course surveys chiropractic and osteopathic manipulation, massage, Rolfing, Trager, craniosacral therapy, Feldenkrais and reflexology.","teach":["The course surveys chiropractic and osteopathic manipulation, massage, Rolfing, Trager, craniosacral therapy, Feldenkrais and reflexology. It defines chiropractic subluxation as joint changes said to compromise nervous-system function. Rolfing is identified as structural integration; Trager uses gentle rhythmic rocking and shaking.","Reflexology maps body parts to areas of the feet within its traditional model. Teach proposed mechanisms and demonstrated outcomes separately."],"keyPoints":["It defines chiropractic subluxation as joint changes said to compromise nervous-system function.","Rolfing is identified as structural integration; Trager uses gentle rhythmic rocking and shaking.","Reflexology maps body parts to areas of the feet within its traditional model."],"visual":{"title":"Bodywork map · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The course surveys chiropractic and osteopathic manipulation, massage, Rolfing, Trager, craniosacral therapy, Feldenkrais and reflexology."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"It defines chiropractic subluxation as joint changes said to compromise nervous-system function."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Rolfing is identified as structural integration; Trager uses gentle rhythmic rocking and shaking."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Reflexology maps body parts to areas of the feet within its traditional model."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"B","explanation":"Use B to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Evidence and scope vary by modality and condition; reflex maps and broad subluxation claims are not established anatomy.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Hydrotherapy and sauna—use safely","highYield":"Hydrotherapy means therapeutic use of water; the source contrasts stimulating cool water with vasodilating warm water.","teach":["Hydrotherapy means therapeutic use of water; the source contrasts stimulating cool water with vasodilating warm water. The lesson describes a traditional sauna as roughly 80–90°C with approximately 15–25 percent relative humidity. Its preferred timing is after exercise, with a cooling interval first.","Hydrate, avoid alcohol, limit duration, cool gradually and leave immediately for dizziness, nausea or illness. Treat detoxification, kidney support, respiratory relief and skin-treatment claims cautiously."],"keyPoints":["The lesson describes a traditional sauna as roughly 80–90°C with approximately 15–25 percent relative humidity.","Its preferred timing is after exercise, with a cooling interval first.","Hydrate, avoid alcohol, limit duration, cool gradually and leave immediately for dizziness, nausea or illness."],"visual":{"title":"Hydrotherapy and sauna—use safely · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Hydrotherapy means therapeutic use of water; the source contrasts stimulating cool water with vasodilating warm water."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The lesson describes a traditional sauna as roughly 80–90°C with approximately 15–25 percent relative humidity."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Its preferred timing is after exercise, with a cooling interval first."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Hydrate, avoid alcohol, limit duration, cool gradually and leave immediately for dizziness, nausea or illness."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"HSS","explanation":"Use HSS to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Sauna is not appropriate for everyone; cardiovascular disease, pregnancy, medicines and impaired heat tolerance may require guidance.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Energy medicine, EFT and evidence","highYield":"The source distinguishes measurable or veritable fields from unmeasured putative fields.","teach":["The source distinguishes measurable or veritable fields from unmeasured putative fields. It places acupuncture, qigong, Reiki, healing touch and intercessory prayer in the putative-field category. Healing touch is described as passing hands over the body to identify and correct an alleged imbalance.","The lesson itself acknowledges that putative fields and therapeutic effects have not been convincingly demonstrated scientifically. Present EFT and related practices as course-described modalities, not replacements for indicated care."],"keyPoints":["It places acupuncture, qigong, Reiki, healing touch and intercessory prayer in the putative-field category.","Healing touch is described as passing hands over the body to identify and correct an alleged imbalance.","The lesson itself acknowledges that putative fields and therapeutic effects have not been convincingly demonstrated scientifically."],"visual":{"title":"Energy medicine, EFT and evidence · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The source distinguishes measurable or veritable fields from unmeasured putative fields."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"It places acupuncture, qigong, Reiki, healing touch and intercessory prayer in the putative-field category."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Healing touch is described as passing hands over the body to identify and correct an alleged imbalance."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The lesson itself acknowledges that putative fields and therapeutic effects have not been convincingly demonstrated scientifically."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"EME","explanation":"Use EME to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"A person may value a ritual or relaxation practice without its proposed energy mechanism being demonstrated.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Biofeedback and CBT: make the invisible visible","highYield":"Biofeedback uses sensors and cues to show measures such as heart rate, breathing, muscle tension, skin temperature or conductivity.","teach":["Biofeedback uses sensors and cues to show measures such as heart rate, breathing, muscle tension, skin temperature or conductivity. The learner practises deliberate changes while observing feedback. Cognitive behavioural therapy links thoughts, beliefs, emotions and behavioural responses.","The course sequence is identify the problem and goal, examine thoughts and beliefs, detect inaccurate patterns, and challenge or revise them. CBT is evidence based, but serious conditions belong with appropriately qualified practitioners."],"keyPoints":["The learner practises deliberate changes while observing feedback.","Cognitive behavioural therapy links thoughts, beliefs, emotions and behavioural responses.","The course sequence is identify the problem and goal, examine thoughts and beliefs, detect inaccurate patterns, and challenge or revise them."],"visual":{"title":"Biofeedback and CBT: make the invisible visible · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Biofeedback uses sensors and cues to show measures such as heart rate, breathing, muscle tension, skin temperature or conductivity."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The learner practises deliberate changes while observing feedback."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Cognitive behavioural therapy links thoughts, beliefs, emotions and behavioural responses."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The course sequence is identify the problem and goal, examine thoughts and beliefs, detect inaccurate patterns, and challenge or revise them."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"BCMIV","explanation":"Use BCMIV to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not reduce distress to “thinking wrong.” CBT includes behaviour, context and collaborative skill-building.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Migraine: recognize, track, prevent","highYield":"Migraine may include one-sided throbbing pain, nausea, sensory sensitivity and sometimes neurological aura.","teach":["Migraine may include one-sided throbbing pain, nausea, sensory sensitivity and sometimes neurological aura. Potential triggers differ by person and may include hormonal change, food, hunger, sleep disruption, stress, weather and strong smells. A diary can connect timing, symptoms, exposures, meals, sleep and medication use without assuming every association is causal.","The article discusses acupuncture, massage, regular sleep, magnesium, riboflavin, butterbur and CoQ10. Butterbur advice is dated: safety depends on PA-free products, and routine use may not be recommended."],"keyPoints":["Potential triggers differ by person and may include hormonal change, food, hunger, sleep disruption, stress, weather and strong smells.","A diary can connect timing, symptoms, exposures, meals, sleep and medication use without assuming every association is causal.","The article discusses acupuncture, massage, regular sleep, magnesium, riboflavin, butterbur and CoQ10."],"visual":{"title":"Migraine: recognize, track, prevent · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Migraine may include one-sided throbbing pain, nausea, sensory sensitivity and sometimes neurological aura."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Potential triggers differ by person and may include hormonal change, food, hunger, sleep disruption, stress, weather and strong smells."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"A diary can connect timing, symptoms, exposures, meals, sleep and medication use without assuming every association is causal."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The article discusses acupuncture, massage, regular sleep, magnesium, riboflavin, butterbur and CoQ10."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"MRTP","explanation":"Use MRTP to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"New severe headache, neurologic deficit, fever, head injury or major pattern change requires urgent assessment.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Natural-healing modalities and osteopathy","highYield":"The natural-healing article profiles naturopathic medicine, Reiki, Bowen therapy, integrated speech and yoga, deep-flow massage, kinesiology, hypnosis, mindfulness and orthomolecular medicine.","teach":["The natural-healing article profiles naturopathic medicine, Reiki, Bowen therapy, integrated speech and yoga, deep-flow massage, kinesiology, hypnosis, mindfulness and orthomolecular medicine. The osteopathy article emphasizes palpation, whole-body relationships, fluid flow and proposed self-healing capacity. Its treatment categories are craniosacral, visceral and musculoskeletal.","The article also stresses referral for imaging, blood work or medical assessment and presents osteopathy as complementary. Case stories illustrate author claims; they do not establish efficacy."],"keyPoints":["The osteopathy article emphasizes palpation, whole-body relationships, fluid flow and proposed self-healing capacity.","Its treatment categories are craniosacral, visceral and musculoskeletal.","The article also stresses referral for imaging, blood work or medical assessment and presents osteopathy as complementary."],"visual":{"title":"Natural-healing modalities and osteopathy · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The natural-healing article profiles naturopathic medicine, Reiki, Bowen therapy, integrated speech and yoga, deep-flow massage, kinesiology, hypnosis, mindfulness and orthomolecular medicine."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The osteopathy article emphasizes palpation, whole-body relationships, fluid flow and proposed self-healing capacity."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Its treatment categories are craniosacral, visceral and musculoskeletal."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The article also stresses referral for imaging, blood work or medical assessment and presents osteopathy as complementary."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"NMO","explanation":"Use NMO to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not treat the modalities as equivalent or assume the same regulation in every jurisdiction.","act":null},{"id":"nhf8-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 8 test and remediation","highYield":"Retrieve the psychoneuroimmunology definition, proper-breathing effects, bodywork distinctions, reflexology and hydrotherapy.","teach":["Retrieve the psychoneuroimmunology definition, proper-breathing effects, bodywork distinctions, reflexology and hydrotherapy. Know the source sauna temperature, humidity, timing and safety rules. Distinguish veritable from putative fields and healing touch from electromagnetic therapy.","Remember that the source keys meditation brain signals as gamma waves. Answer source-faithfully, then revisit every immune, detoxification, subluxation and energy-field claim through an evidence lens."],"keyPoints":["Know the source sauna temperature, humidity, timing and safety rules.","Distinguish veritable from putative fields and healing touch from electromagnetic therapy.","Remember that the source keys meditation brain signals as gamma waves."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 8 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Retrieve the psychoneuroimmunology definition, proper-breathing effects, bodywork distinctions, reflexology and hydrotherapy."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Know the source sauna temperature, humidity, timing and safety rules."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Distinguish veritable from putative fields and healing touch from electromagnetic therapy."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Remember that the source keys meditation brain signals as gamma waves."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The supplied printer test has no official answer key; course answers are inferred and evidence flags remain separate.","act":null},{"id":"mind-body-match","title":"Match it: The mind–body loop · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system.","Mind–body medicine integrates physical, mental, emotional and spiritual dimensions in its model of health.","The lesson groups breathing, meditation, bodywork, biofeedback and cognitive-behavioural methods under this umbrella.","Separate the broad mind–body principle from stronger claims that a specific practice treats a specific disease."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Separate the broad mind–body principle from stronger claims that a specific practice treats a specific disease."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: The lesson groups breathing, meditation, bodywork, biofeedback and cognitive-behavioural methods under this umbrella."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: Mind–body medicine integrates physical, mental, emotional and spiritual dimensions in its model of health."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"The mind–body loop","back":"The course defines psychoneuroimmunology as the study of connections among emotions, the nervous system and the immune system."},{"front":"Breathe to shift state","back":"Shallow chest breathing is associated in the lesson with fight-or-flight, while diaphragmatic breathing is presented as calming."},{"front":"Meditation: train attention and physiology","back":"Meditation is quiet contemplation focused on breath, a phrase, a mantra, a theme or present-moment experience."},{"front":"Bodywork map","back":"The course surveys chiropractic and osteopathic manipulation, massage, Rolfing, Trager, craniosacral therapy, Feldenkrais and reflexology."},{"front":"Hydrotherapy and sauna—use safely","back":"Hydrotherapy means therapeutic use of water; the source contrasts stimulating cool water with vasodilating warm water."},{"front":"Energy medicine, EFT and evidence","back":"The source distinguishes measurable or veritable fields from unmeasured putative fields."},{"front":"Biofeedback and CBT: make the invisible visible","back":"Biofeedback uses sensors and cues to show measures such as heart rate, breathing, muscle tension, skin temperature or conductivity."},{"front":"Migraine: recognize, track, prevent","back":"Migraine may include one-sided throbbing pain, nausea, sensory sensitivity and sometimes neurological aura."},{"front":"Natural-healing modalities and osteopathy","back":"The natural-healing article profiles naturopathic medicine, Reiki, Bowen therapy, integrated speech and yoga, deep-flow massage, kinesiology, hypnosis, mindfulness and orthomolecular medicine."},{"front":"Official Lesson 8 test and remediation","back":"Retrieve the psychoneuroimmunology definition, proper-breathing effects, bodywork distinctions, reflexology and hydrotherapy."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Psychoneuroimmunology is the study of the connections between:","o":["the emotions, the brain, and the lymphatic system","the nervous system, the brain, and the cardiovascular system","the immune system, the digestive system, and the nervous system","the nervous system, the immune system, and the emotions","Both A and C"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Mind-body medicine includes practices such as:","o":["meditation","yoga","osteopathy","massage therapy","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":""},{"q":"Full, deep breathing results in:","o":["the flight-or-fight response","hyperventilation","breathing through the mouth","air filling the diaphragm","Both C and D"],"a":3,"e":"The source wording is anatomically imprecise: the diaphragm is a muscle; the lungs fill as the diaphragm contracts."},{"q":"Proper breathing can:","o":["improve nervous system function","decrease anxiety and depression","reduce fatigue","all of the above","Both A and C"],"a":3,"e":""},{"q":"Subluxation is:","o":["a form of massage therapy","a complex of changes to joints that compromise optimal function of the nervous system","a symptom of high blood pressure","a technique used in craniosacral therapy","both B and D"],"a":1,"e":"This is the course/chiropractic definition; broad subluxation mechanisms are contested and should not be presented as established pathology."},{"q":"Meditation can be used to:","o":["boost the immune system","lower blood pressure","improve circulation","all of the above","Both A and B"],"a":3,"e":"Course answer. Benefits are outcome- and population-specific; “boost immunity” is vague."},{"q":"In the practice of _________________, all parts of the body may be mapped to the feet.","o":["chiropractics","shiatsu","watsu","rolfing","reflexology"],"a":4,"e":"This describes the reflexology model, not established anatomic mapping."},{"q":"____________________ means healing with water.","o":["hydrology","hydrotherapy","watsu","sauna","Tui Nai"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Examples of energy medicine involving putative energy fields include:","o":["electromagnetic therapy","acupuncture","intercessory prayer","all of the above","Both B and C"],"a":4,"e":"This is the source classification; putative fields are not demonstrated measurable anatomy."},{"q":"The health benefits of sauna include:","o":["improving circulation","supporting detoxification","relieving respiratory problems","improving skin problems","all of the above"],"a":4,"e":"Course answer. “Detoxification,” respiratory and skin-treatment claims require caution and do not apply universally."},{"q":"Chiropractic discipline deal with all of the following, EXCEPT:","o":["bones","Visceral systems","Joints","Muscles","Spines"],"a":1,"e":"Official wording is grammatically inconsistent; source-intended answer is visceral systems."},{"q":"Diana has bought a home sauna; when’s the optimal time for her to use it?","o":["Half hour before bedtime","Half hour after eating","Just before eating","Half hour after exercising","First thing in the morning"],"a":3,"e":"Source preference rather than a universal rule; health status, heat tolerance and hydration matter."},{"q":"Electrical signals in the brain stimulated by meditation are also known as:","o":["alpha waves","psychotronic signals","gamma waves","muons","pneuronic signals"],"a":2,"e":"The lesson explicitly keys gamma waves. Meditation research also discusses other rhythms; the wording is imprecise."},{"q":"_________________________________ is a form of energy medicine in which a therapist passes his or her hands over the patient’s body to identify and correct imbalances.","o":["tai chi","healing touch","shiatsu","electromagnetic healing"],"a":1,"e":"This describes the healing-touch model; the proposed energy imbalance is not established biomedically."},{"q":"Generally, the temperature in a sauna should reach:","o":["80 to 90 C","175 to 195 C","65 to 75 C","80 to 90 F","35 to 40 C"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Cold water dilates blood vessels, improving blood circulation.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Trager bodywork is also known as structural integration.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Rolfing, not Trager, is commonly called structural integration."},{"q":"Misalignments in the spine may impede blood and nutrient supply in the body.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer. Broad vascular or nutrient-supply claims from spinal misalignment are weakly supported."},{"q":"A massage may help boost the immune system and relieve respiratory conditions.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer. “Boost immunity” and respiratory-treatment claims are nonspecific and evidence is limited."},{"q":"The relative humidity in most saunas is about 80 to 90 percent.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""},{"q":"Sweating supports the kidneys.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer. Sweating does not replace renal regulation or broadly “detoxify” the body."},{"q":"Meditation supports the immune system, increasing our body's natural defence against tumours, viruses, colds, flu, and other infectious diseases.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Course answer significantly overstates current evidence; meditation is not a cancer or infection treatment."},{"q":"The abdomen and chest should expand with each breath.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Directionally consistent with relaxed breathing, though individual mechanics vary."},{"q":"Meditation is part of the traditions of Christianity and Islam.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":""},{"q":"Chiropractors treat spinal misalignments but cannot help peripheral joints.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":""}],"caseStudy":null},{"n":9,"title":"Traditional Holistic Healing Practices","kicker":"Compare Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and Indigenous healing teachings with cultural humility and clear evidence boundaries.","steps":[{"id":"nhf9-1","chapter":"Traditions, comparison and respectful practice","chapterSummary":null,"title":"Three healing traditions, one comparison lens","highYield":"The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions.","teach":["The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions. It identifies balance, prevention, mind–body–spirit integration and proposed self-healing capacity as recurring themes.","Each system has its own history, language, practices and cultural authority; they are not interchangeable. Traditional explanatory models should be distinguished from biomedical anatomy and causal evidence."],"keyPoints":["It identifies balance, prevention, mind–body–spirit integration and proposed self-healing capacity as recurring themes.","Each system has its own history, language, practices and cultural authority; they are not interchangeable.","Traditional explanatory models should be distinguished from biomedical anatomy and causal evidence."],"visual":{"title":"Three healing traditions, one comparison lens · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"It identifies balance, prevention, mind–body–spirit integration and proposed self-healing capacity as recurring themes."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Each system has its own history, language, practices and cultural authority; they are not interchangeable."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Traditional explanatory models should be distinguished from biomedical anatomy and causal evidence."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"THTCL","explanation":"Use THTCL to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"“Traditional” is not one universal category. Name the tradition and community, and avoid extracting sacred knowledge from its context.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-2","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Ayurveda: elements, doshas and constitution","highYield":"Ayurveda translates as science of life and is presented as an ancient Indian system of living, prevention and healing.","teach":["Ayurveda translates as science of life and is presented as an ancient Indian system of living, prevention and healing. Its five elements are earth, water, fire, air and ether. Its three doshas are vata, pitta and kapha; prakriti refers to an individual constitutional pattern.","The source says care is individualized to physical, mental, lifestyle and environmental conditions. Doshas and elemental constitutions are traditional models, not biomedical diagnoses."],"keyPoints":["Its five elements are earth, water, fire, air and ether.","Its three doshas are vata, pitta and kapha; prakriti refers to an individual constitutional pattern.","The source says care is individualized to physical, mental, lifestyle and environmental conditions."],"visual":{"title":"Ayurveda: elements, doshas and constitution · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Ayurveda translates as science of life and is presented as an ancient Indian system of living, prevention and healing."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Its five elements are earth, water, fire, air and ether."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Its three doshas are vata, pitta and kapha; prakriti refers to an individual constitutional pattern."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The source says care is individualized to physical, mental, lifestyle and environmental conditions."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"AEDC","explanation":"Use AEDC to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Respect cultural meaning while avoiding disease predictions based solely on dosha typing.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-3","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"The six tastes and Ayurvedic care","highYield":"The six tastes are sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter and astringent.","teach":["The six tastes are sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter and astringent. For the source test: sour stimulates digestion; salty increases pitta and kapha; pungent is stimulating and warming; bitter is said to stimulate digestion and absorb phlegm; astringent increases vata and decreases pitta and kapha. The source describes medicinal plants, diet rules, exercise, massage, yoga, breathing, meditation and lifestyle change as care components.","Prana, agni and soma are the three—not four—primal forces named by the lesson. Taste–dosha effects are course teachings, not established nutritional physiology."],"keyPoints":["For the source test: sour stimulates digestion; salty increases pitta and kapha; pungent is stimulating and warming; bitter is said to stimulate digestion and absorb phlegm; astringent increases vata and decreases pitta and kapha.","The source describes medicinal plants, diet rules, exercise, massage, yoga, breathing, meditation and lifestyle change as care components.","Prana, agni and soma are the three—not four—primal forces named by the lesson."],"visual":{"title":"The six tastes and Ayurvedic care · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The six tastes are sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter and astringent."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"For the source test: sour stimulates digestion; salty increases pitta and kapha; pungent is stimulating and warming; bitter is said to stimulate digestion and absorb phlegm; astringent increases vata and decreases pitta and kapha."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The source describes medicinal plants, diet rules, exercise, massage, yoga, breathing, meditation and lifestyle change as care components."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Prana, agni and soma are the three—not four—primal forces named by the lesson."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"TAC","explanation":"Use TAC to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"A traditional classification can guide cultural food practice without being presented as a biochemical law.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-4","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"TCM: yin–yang and Five Phases","highYield":"The lesson describes traditional Chinese medicine as restoring balance, with herbs supported by acupuncture and massage.","teach":["The lesson describes traditional Chinese medicine as restoring balance, with herbs supported by acupuncture and massage. Chinese medicines are grouped in the source as superior, middle and inferior. Yin is described as female, dark and cold; yang as male, light and hot.","The Five Phases are wood, fire, earth, metal and water, each paired with yin and yang organ categories. These are traditional relational models rather than biomedical organ physiology."],"keyPoints":["Chinese medicines are grouped in the source as superior, middle and inferior.","Yin is described as female, dark and cold; yang as male, light and hot.","The Five Phases are wood, fire, earth, metal and water, each paired with yin and yang organ categories."],"visual":{"title":"TCM: yin–yang and Five Phases · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The lesson describes traditional Chinese medicine as restoring balance, with herbs supported by acupuncture and massage."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Chinese medicines are grouped in the source as superior, middle and inferior."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Yin is described as female, dark and cold; yang as male, light and hot."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The Five Phases are wood, fire, earth, metal and water, each paired with yin and yang organ categories."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"TYFP","explanation":"Use TYFP to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Do not use symbolic organ categories as a substitute for medical diagnosis of liver, heart, kidney or other disease.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-5","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Qi, meridians and acupuncture","highYield":"Qi is presented as vital energy moving through pathways called meridians.","teach":["Qi is presented as vital energy moving through pathways called meridians. The source teaches 12 main meridians and eight extraordinary meridians. Named main meridians include the large intestine, spleen and liver.","Acupuncture is described as stimulating points by needles, heat, pressure or massage to rebalance qi. Meridians are elements of the TCM model and should not be represented as demonstrated anatomical structures."],"keyPoints":["The source teaches 12 main meridians and eight extraordinary meridians.","Named main meridians include the large intestine, spleen and liver.","Acupuncture is described as stimulating points by needles, heat, pressure or massage to rebalance qi."],"visual":{"title":"Qi, meridians and acupuncture · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Qi is presented as vital energy moving through pathways called meridians."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"The source teaches 12 main meridians and eight extraordinary meridians."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Named main meridians include the large intestine, spleen and liver."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Acupuncture is described as stimulating points by needles, heat, pressure or massage to rebalance qi."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"MA","explanation":"Use MA to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Evidence for acupuncture is condition- and outcome-specific; proposed qi mechanisms are distinct from clinical effect research.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-6","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Qigong and tai chi in practice","highYield":"Tai chi combines controlled movement, breathing, concentration and martial-arts roots.","teach":["Tai chi combines controlled movement, breathing, concentration and martial-arts roots. Qigong combines slow movement with regulated breathing, meditation, self-massage or imagery. The qigong article identifies healing or medical, external-qi, spiritual and martial applications.","Movement, balance, flexibility, stress reduction and quality of life are reasonable themes. Claims involving cancer, immunity, stroke mortality, medication reduction or disease reversal need modern evidence review."],"keyPoints":["Qigong combines slow movement with regulated breathing, meditation, self-massage or imagery.","The qigong article identifies healing or medical, external-qi, spiritual and martial applications.","Movement, balance, flexibility, stress reduction and quality of life are reasonable themes."],"visual":{"title":"Qigong and tai chi in practice · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Tai chi combines controlled movement, breathing, concentration and martial-arts roots."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Qigong combines slow movement with regulated breathing, meditation, self-massage or imagery."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The qigong article identifies healing or medical, external-qi, spiritual and martial applications."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Movement, balance, flexibility, stress reduction and quality of life are reasonable themes."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"QP","explanation":"Use QP to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Low-impact movement can be valuable without proving traditional energy explanations or broad disease claims.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-7","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Indigenous healing and the Medicine Wheel","highYield":"The source presents healing as physical, spiritual, relational and connected with nature and the cosmos.","teach":["The source presents healing as physical, spiritual, relational and connected with nature and the cosmos. Its test mapping is East–Eagle–yellow–new beginnings; South–Beaver–red–playfulness; West–Turtle–black–transformation; North–Wolf–white–wisdom and justice. The circle represents connectedness and balance.","The source notes that symbols and teachings vary among Indigenous Nations. Use official source terms only for test fidelity; teaching should say Indigenous or name the specific Nation and avoid presenting one wheel as universal."],"keyPoints":["Its test mapping is East–Eagle–yellow–new beginnings; South–Beaver–red–playfulness; West–Turtle–black–transformation; North–Wolf–white–wisdom and justice.","The circle represents connectedness and balance.","The source notes that symbols and teachings vary among Indigenous Nations."],"visual":{"title":"Indigenous healing and the Medicine Wheel · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The source presents healing as physical, spiritual, relational and connected with nature and the cosmos."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Its test mapping is East–Eagle–yellow–new beginnings; South–Beaver–red–playfulness; West–Turtle–black–transformation; North–Wolf–white–wisdom and justice."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"The circle represents connectedness and balance."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"The source notes that symbols and teachings vary among Indigenous Nations."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"IHMW","explanation":"Use IHMW to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The PDF title and test use outdated pan-Indigenous terminology. Preserve it only where quoting the source, and teach with specificity and respect.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-8","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Four sacred plants—with cultural respect","highYield":"The article discusses sage, sweetgrass, cedar and tobacco as four culturally significant plants.","teach":["The article discusses sage, sweetgrass, cedar and tobacco as four culturally significant plants. It describes ceremonial and traditional topical or medicinal uses. Sweetgrass is identified as toxic if consumed; cedar preparations and essential oils require careful identification and dosing.","Commercial tobacco use is harmful and addictive and must not be conflated with ceremonial use. Sacred-plant knowledge should not be detached from Nation, community authority, protocol or consent."],"keyPoints":["It describes ceremonial and traditional topical or medicinal uses.","Sweetgrass is identified as toxic if consumed; cedar preparations and essential oils require careful identification and dosing.","Commercial tobacco use is harmful and addictive and must not be conflated with ceremonial use."],"visual":{"title":"Four sacred plants—with cultural respect · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The article discusses sage, sweetgrass, cedar and tobacco as four culturally significant plants."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"It describes ceremonial and traditional topical or medicinal uses."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Sweetgrass is identified as toxic if consumed; cedar preparations and essential oils require careful identification and dosing."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Commercial tobacco use is harmful and addictive and must not be conflated with ceremonial use."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"FSPCR","explanation":"Use FSPCR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Cultural significance is not permission for commercialization or self-treatment. Plant safety still depends on species, route and dose.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-9","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Six approaches to natural pain relief","highYield":"The pain article begins with nociception: peripheral signals travel through the spinal cord to the brain.","teach":["The pain article begins with nociception: peripheral signals travel through the spinal cord to the brain. It compares yoga, acupuncture, Bowen therapy, massage, chiropractic and reflexology. Each modality is organized around proposed mechanism, cited research and claimed benefits.","Evidence is not equally strong across modalities or conditions; case reports and small studies are not equivalent to robust trials. Persistent, severe, progressive, traumatic, neurological or unexplained pain requires appropriate assessment."],"keyPoints":["It compares yoga, acupuncture, Bowen therapy, massage, chiropractic and reflexology.","Each modality is organized around proposed mechanism, cited research and claimed benefits.","Evidence is not equally strong across modalities or conditions; case reports and small studies are not equivalent to robust trials."],"visual":{"title":"Six approaches to natural pain relief · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"The pain article begins with nociception: peripheral signals travel through the spinal cord to the brain."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"It compares yoga, acupuncture, Bowen therapy, massage, chiropractic and reflexology."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Each modality is organized around proposed mechanism, cited research and claimed benefits."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Evidence is not equally strong across modalities or conditions; case reports and small studies are not equivalent to robust trials."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"ANPR","explanation":"Use ANPR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"Pain is both physiologic and contextual, but that does not make it imaginary or prove any one mind–body treatment.","act":null},{"id":"nhf9-10","chapter":null,"chapterSummary":null,"title":"Official Lesson 9 test and remediation","highYield":"Retrieve the five TCM phases and organ relationships, yin versus yang and the 12 main meridians.","teach":["Retrieve the five TCM phases and organ relationships, yin versus yang and the 12 main meridians. Know the three doshas, six Ayurvedic tastes, three primal forces and individualized-care factors. Practise the source Medicine Wheel direction, animal and colour associations without universalizing them.","Distinguish traditional system descriptions from biomedical claims. Use the inferred source key for assessment fidelity, then review cultural and evidence flags before applying any claim."],"keyPoints":["Know the three doshas, six Ayurvedic tastes, three primal forces and individualized-care factors.","Practise the source Medicine Wheel direction, animal and colour associations without universalizing them.","Distinguish traditional system descriptions from biomedical claims."],"visual":{"title":"Official Lesson 9 test and remediation · connection map","items":[{"label":"Notice","detail":"Retrieve the five TCM phases and organ relationships, yin versus yang and the 12 main meridians."},{"label":"Connect","detail":"Know the three doshas, six Ayurvedic tastes, three primal forces and individualized-care factors."},{"label":"Distinguish","detail":"Practise the source Medicine Wheel direction, animal and colour associations without universalizing them."},{"label":"Apply","detail":"Distinguish traditional system descriptions from biomedical claims."}]},"mnemonic":{"cue":"OLTR","explanation":"Use OLTR to rebuild the idea, then name its evidence or cultural boundary."},"goodToKnow":"The printer test has no official answer key. Several questions reproduce culturally broad or weak-evidence source claims; their notes remain visible.","act":null},{"id":"traditional-systems-match","title":"Match it: Three healing traditions, one comparison lens · connection map","teach":[],"act":{"type":"sort","variant":"match","prompt":"Drag each term onto what it describes.","categories":["The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions.","It identifies balance, prevention, mind–body–spirit integration and proposed self-healing capacity as recurring themes.","Each system has its own history, language, practices and cultural authority; they are not interchangeable.","Traditional explanatory models should be distinguished from biomedical anatomy and causal evidence."],"items":[{"label":"Apply","category":3,"feedback":"Apply: Traditional explanatory models should be distinguished from biomedical anatomy and causal evidence."},{"label":"Distinguish","category":2,"feedback":"Distinguish: Each system has its own history, language, practices and cultural authority; they are not interchangeable."},{"label":"Connect","category":1,"feedback":"Connect: It identifies balance, prevention, mind–body–spirit integration and proposed self-healing capacity as recurring themes."},{"label":"Notice","category":0,"feedback":"Notice: The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions."}]}}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Three healing traditions, one comparison lens","back":"The lesson introduces Ayurveda, traditional Chinese medicine and North American Indigenous healing traditions."},{"front":"Ayurveda: elements, doshas and constitution","back":"Ayurveda translates as science of life and is presented as an ancient Indian system of living, prevention and healing."},{"front":"The six tastes and Ayurvedic care","back":"The six tastes are sweet, sour, salty, pungent, bitter and astringent."},{"front":"TCM: yin–yang and Five Phases","back":"The lesson describes traditional Chinese medicine as restoring balance, with herbs supported by acupuncture and massage."},{"front":"Qi, meridians and acupuncture","back":"Qi is presented as vital energy moving through pathways called meridians."},{"front":"Qigong and tai chi in practice","back":"Tai chi combines controlled movement, breathing, concentration and martial-arts roots."},{"front":"Indigenous healing and the Medicine Wheel","back":"The source presents healing as physical, spiritual, relational and connected with nature and the cosmos."},{"front":"Four sacred plants—with cultural respect","back":"The article discusses sage, sweetgrass, cedar and tobacco as four culturally significant plants."},{"front":"Six approaches to natural pain relief","back":"The pain article begins with nociception: peripheral signals travel through the spinal cord to the brain."},{"front":"Official Lesson 9 test and remediation","back":"Retrieve the five TCM phases and organ relationships, yin versus yang and the 12 main meridians."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The Five Phase Theory:","o":["includes yin and yang as two of the main five phases","consists of the use of three of the five medicinal plants (fresh, dried, or naturally extracted)","focuses on strict diet guidelines and exercise as the two main components of the theory","is a philosophy of the five elements and their relationship to body organs","includes five elements: earth, water, fire, air, and ether"],"a":3,"e":"This is a traditional relational model, not biomedical organ physiology."},{"q":"Ayurveda tailors treatments according to the uniqueness of all of the following aspects of an individual EXCEPT the person’s ______________________________.","o":["physical condition","mental condition","financial condition","lifestyle condition","environmental condition"],"a":2,"e":"Course-framework answer; Ayurveda is a traditional system, not a biomedical diagnostic method."},{"q":"According to Ayurvedic medicine sour foods stimulate:","o":["toxins","digestion","phlegm","warming","body fluids"],"a":1,"e":"Source doctrine, not established nutritional physiology."},{"q":"Chinese medicines are classified and divided into three categories:","o":["early, mid, and advanced","herbs, plants, and flowers","warm, light, and dry","superior, middle, and inferior","sour, bitter, and salty"],"a":3,"e":"Traditional source classification."},{"q":"Ayurveda is an ancient system from India that seeks to promote health _______________________________________________.","o":["by creating a balance in the doshas within and around us","by acupuncture and massage","through the philosophy of opposites and balance","through the philosophy of the five elements","using the best of African, Middle Eastern, and South American healing techniques"],"a":0,"e":"Doshas are a traditional Ayurvedic model, not biomedical anatomy."},{"q":"A ________ taste increases pitta and kapha.","o":["sweet","bitter","pungent","salty","astringent"],"a":3,"e":"Source doctrine, not established physiology."},{"q":"Pungent foods are:","o":["used to treat heavy menstruation","used to cleanse digestive organs","useful for fevers and skin disease","believed to increase body fluids","stimulating and warming"],"a":4,"e":"Traditional source description."},{"q":"Ayurvedic treatment may consist of the use of:","o":["heating or cooling herbs","medicinal plants, strict diet guidelines, and exercise","the four directions","acupuncture, herbs, and balance","the great rishis"],"a":1,"e":"Course answer describes traditional practice; safety and evidence depend on the specific intervention."},{"q":"Native American healing practices tie the human body to:","o":["positive internal energy","balancing the five pillars","understanding how nature impacts the earth and our internal body","the cosmos","earth, wind, and fire"],"a":3,"e":"Official source wording is retained for test fidelity. Use “Indigenous” or the specific Nation in teaching; traditions are not universal."},{"q":"The medicine wheel states that West provides the medicine of the _________________.","o":["Wolf","Beaver","Turtle","Eagle","Raven"],"a":2,"e":"This is the source’s particular mapping; Medicine Wheel teachings vary among Nations."},{"q":"Studies have shown that ___________ can have beneficial effects on the body, although they have not been able to fully explain how it works within the framework of Western medicine.","o":["herbalism","chakras","kapha","vata","acupuncture"],"a":4,"e":"Clinical evidence for acupuncture varies by condition and outcome; qi/meridian explanations are traditional models."},{"q":"Diagnostic tools are used in _______________ medicine and include taking an analysis of body mind, spirit, and environment.","o":["Native American","Ayurvedic","Chinese","African","Middle Eastern"],"a":1,"e":"Traditional course description, not a biomedical diagnostic standard."},{"q":"In Native American healing the Wolf:","o":["brings wisdom and justice","has the ability of looking far afield","has the power of transformation and change","symbolizes the place of new beginnings","has an environment they share with anyone"],"a":0,"e":"Source-specific association; avoid presenting it as universal across Indigenous Nations."},{"q":"In acupuncture, there are thought to be ____ main meridians forming an energy circuit throughout the body.","o":["365","5","12","15 to 20","4"],"a":2,"e":"Meridians are part of the TCM model and are not demonstrated anatomic structures."},{"q":"The main meridians include:","o":["heart, circulatory system, and lungs","eyes, heart, and soul","digestive system, stomach, and colon","mind, heart, and soul","large intestine, spleen and liver"],"a":4,"e":"Traditional TCM categories rather than biomedical organ pathways."},{"q":"In acupuncture, each meridian is associated with an organ and when an organ is removed it must be replaced by another meridian.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"False within the source framework; meridians are not anatomic replacement structures."},{"q":"Ayurvedic medicine assists nature by supporting beneficial processes to reinstate and maintain proper balance of doshas.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True as a description of the Ayurvedic model, not a biomedical mechanism."},{"q":"Bitter foods stimulate the digestion, absorb phlegm, and cleanse digestive toxins.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Source doctrine; “digestive toxins” and dosha/taste actions are not established physiology."},{"q":"In yin and yang, the philosophy of opposites and balance, yin is seen as light and hot.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"Within the source, yang is light and hot; yin is dark and cold."},{"q":"In Native American healing, the South is the place of light heartedness and playfulness.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"Source-specific mapping; Medicine Wheel teachings vary among Nations."},{"q":"Each of the Five Phase Theory elements is closely related to two (yin and yang) body organs.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True within the source’s traditional model, not biomedical organ physiology."},{"q":"The colour of the West is blue representing water to help us to go inside, take a truthful look at ourselves, and make the changes needed to achieve balance.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"False in the source mapping; its West is black. These mappings are not universal."},{"q":"An astringent taste increases vata and decreases pitta and kapha.","o":["True","False"],"a":0,"e":"True within the source’s Ayurvedic model, not established physiology."},{"q":"At the centre of Ayurvedic belief are four primal forces: prana, the breath of life; agni, the spirit of fire and light; doshas that balance with the individual’s constitutional type, and soma, the spirit of harmony and love.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The source names three primal forces—prana, agni and soma; doshas are a separate concept."},{"q":"In traditional Chinese medicine (TCM), illness implies that the balance within the whole system has been distributed evenly.","o":["True","False"],"a":1,"e":"The source describes illness as imbalance or disrupted harmony, not evenly distributed balance."}],"caseStudy":null}]};
